            <title><![CDATA[Introducing OpenFatigue (151 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://openfatigue.ai/</link>
            <guid>50061278</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 19:08:21 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://openfatigue.ai/">https://openfatigue.ai/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50061278">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div id="main">
    <!-- HERO -->
    <div id="top">
      <p>Introducing OpenFatigue™</p>
      
      <p>
        OpenChamber. OpenAI. Open WebUI. OpenDoor. OpenTable. OpenClaw. Open weights.
        Open this. Open that. The word has been opened so many times it no longer closes.
        <strong>OpenFatigue™</strong> is the first fully&nbsp;Open-branded platform for people who are
        <em>exhausted</em> by fully&nbsp;Open-branded platforms.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="#is-it-open">Is it actually open? →</a>
      </p>
      <p>*Free as in “free trial.” Not free as in freedom. Not free as in beer. Free as in “we'll email you.”</p>
      </div>

    <!-- LOGO WALL -->
    <section aria-labelledby="logos-h">
      <h2 id="logos-h">Trusted by companies who are also Open™</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>OpenAI</li>
        <li>OpenTable</li>
        <li>Opendoor</li>
        <li>Open WebUI</li>
        <li>OpenChamber</li>
        <li>OpenClaw</li>
        <li>Open Weights</li>
        <li>OpenThis</li>
        <li>OpenThat</li>
        <li>OpenOpen</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Logos withheld to remain open about our closed relationship with trademark law. None of these companies endorse us. Most have never heard of us. That's fine. We're tired.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- FEATURES -->
    <section id="features" aria-labelledby="features-h">
      <p>Features</p>
      <h2 id="features-h">Everything you expect from Open.<br><span>Nothing you expect from open.</span></h2>
      <div>
        <article>
          
          <h3>Open-ish Licensing</h3>
          <p>Our license is open for anyone to read. Using, modifying, or distributing the software is a separate conversation with Legal.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          
          <h3>Weights Available*</h3>
          <p>Download our model weights after agreeing to a 41-page acceptable use policy, a monthly-active-user cap, and a vibe check.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          
          <h3>Source Visible</h3>
          <p>We publish a high-resolution screenshot of our source code every quarter. Pull requests may be submitted by fax.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          
          <h3>Community Edition</h3>
          <p>Built by a vibrant community of one (1) maintainer, currently on sabbatical. Enterprise Edition includes the other features.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          
          <h3>Open Door Policy</h3>
          <p>Our door is always open. The building is badge-access only. The badge reader is closed source.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          
          <h3>Open Roadmap</h3>
          <p>Our roadmap is completely public, entirely aspirational, and subject to change by a non-profit board that may or may not exist next week.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- METER -->
    <section id="meter" aria-labelledby="meter-h">
      <p>The Openness Meter™</p>
      <h2 id="meter-h">How open is <em>your</em> Open?</h2>
      <p>Drag the slider to calibrate your openness to industry standards.</p>
      
    </section>

    <!-- GENERATOR -->
    <section id="generator" aria-labelledby="gen-h">
      <p>Open Name Generator™</p>
      <h2 id="gen-h">Raise a seed round in one click.</h2>
      <p>Every great company starts with “Open” and a noun. We automated the noun.</p>
      <div>
        <p id="gen-name" aria-live="polite">OpenFatigue</p>
        <dl>
          <p><dt>Tagline</dt><dd id="gen-tagline">The open platform for being tired.</dd></p>
          <p><dt>License</dt><dd id="gen-license">Open License™ (All Rights Reserved)</dd></p>
          <p><dt>Actual openness</dt><dd id="gen-score">3%</dd></p>
          <p><dt>Valuation</dt><dd id="gen-valuation">$1.2B</dd></p>
        </dl>
        
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- IS IT OPEN -->
    <section id="is-it-open" aria-labelledby="iio-h">
      <p>Is It Open?™</p>
      <h2 id="iio-h">Our proprietary openness classifier.</h2>
      <p>Enter any product. Our closed-source model will tell you how open it is.</p>
      
      
    </section>

    <!-- PRICING -->
    <section id="pricing" aria-labelledby="pricing-h">
      <p>Pricing</p>
      <h2 id="pricing-h">Open pricing.<sup>†</sup></h2>
      <div>
        <article>
          <h3>Open</h3>
          <p>$0<span>/mo*</span></p>
          <ul>
            <li>Read the README</li>
            <li>Star the repo (required)</li>
            <li>Join the Discord (read-only)</li>
            <li>Run the software</li>
          </ul>
          
        </article>
        <article>
          <p>Most Open</p>
          <h3>Open Pro</h3>
          <p>$49<span>/mo/seat/token/vibe</span></p>
          <ul>
            <li>Everything in Open</li>
            <li>Weights, with a 41-page AUP</li>
            <li>“Open” in your email signature</li>
            <li>Commercial use under 700M MAU</li>
          </ul>
          
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Open Enterprise</h3>
          <p>Contact<span> Sales</span></p>
          <ul>
            <li>Everything in Open Pro</li>
            <li>See the source (in a room, supervised)</li>
            <li>A dedicated Openness Success Manager</li>
            <li>We'll stop calling it open, upon request</li>
          </ul>
          
        </article>
      </div>
      <p>† Pricing is open to interpretation. * Excludes inference, storage, egress, ingress, regress, tokens, the tokens used to count tokens, and vibes.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- TESTIMONIALS -->
    <section aria-labelledby="t-h">
      <p>Testimonials</p>
      <h2 id="t-h">Loved by people who are very, very tired.</h2>
      <div>
        <figure>
          <blockquote>“I tried to make a restaurant reservation and accidentally fine-tuned a language model. OpenFatigue finally made me feel seen.”</blockquote>
          <figcaption>— Dana K., Fictional Person</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <blockquote>“My open-source project got forked into a closed product called OpenMyProject. Now I just lie on the floor.”</blockquote>
          <figcaption>— Sam R., Maintainer (unpaid)</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <blockquote>“We renamed our company from ClosedCorp to OpenCorp. Changed nothing else. Series B closed in 48 hours.”</blockquote>
          <figcaption>— Chad, Founder &amp; Chief Openness Officer</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <blockquote>“I bought a house through an app called Open-something and now I don't know if I own a home or a model checkpoint.”</blockquote>
          <figcaption>— Priya M., Homeowner (probably)</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <blockquote>“The OSI definition is right there. It's been right there since 1998. Nobody reads it. I read it every night.”</blockquote>
          <figcaption>— Anonymous, Open Source Definition Enjoyer</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <blockquote>“10/10. Would open again.”</blockquote>
          <figcaption>— OpenClaw (a claw)</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SYMPTOMS -->
    <section id="symptoms" aria-labelledby="s-h">
      <p>Symptoms</p>
      <h2 id="s-h">Do you have OpenFatigue?</h2>
      <p>Consult your nearest four-freedoms practitioner if symptoms persist for more than one funding cycle.</p>
      <div>
        <details>
          <summary>You flinch when a press release says “open.”</summary>
          <p>This is the most common early symptom. Your brain has learned that “open” now precedes words like “waitlist,” “API key,” and “enterprise tier.” This is a healthy defense mechanism.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>You've started checking the LICENSE file before the landing page.</summary>
          <p>Congratulations — you are developing immunity. Look for phrases like “acceptable use,” “non-commercial,” “field of use,” and “we reserve the right.” None of those are open source. Real open source licenses let you use, study, modify, and share — for any purpose.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>You can no longer tell if “Open___” is a startup, a door, or a restaurant.</summary>
          <p>Neither can we. One of them sells houses, one books dinner, one serves models, one is a chamber, one is a claw. We stopped asking. Please do not open the claw.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>You say “open weights” with air quotes, out loud, unprompted.</summary>
          <p>Weights without training data, training code, or a permissive license are a binary you're allowed to download. It's nice! It's just… a binary you're allowed to download.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>So what does “open source” actually mean?</summary>
          <p>The term has a <a href="https://opensource.org/osd" rel="noopener">specific, published definition</a> from the Open Source Initiative, and there's also an <a href="https://opensource.org/ai/open-source-ai-definition" rel="noopener">Open Source AI Definition</a>. Supporting projects that meet them is the only known cure for OpenFatigue.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FINAL CTA -->
    <section>
      <h2>Ready to open up?</h2>
      <p>Join <span id="counter">8,412,903</span> people who have clicked a button labeled “Open” and found a form.</p>
      
    </section>
  </div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
