            <title><![CDATA[Turn a WiFi 7 Router into a 320Msps Software Defined Radio Receiver (205 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://github.com/h0m3us3r/w1700k-sdr</link>
            <guid>50055593</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 12:41:58 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://github.com/h0m3us3r/w1700k-sdr">https://github.com/h0m3us3r/w1700k-sdr</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50055593">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div data-hpc="true" containertiming="hpc"><article itemprop="text">
<p dir="auto">Turns a Gemtek W1700K Wi-Fi 7 router into a wideband software-defined receiver.
The router's MediaTek MT7996 radio captures raw 14-bit IQ at up to 320 Msps,
its Airoha AN7581 SoC streams it over 10 GbE, and a Linux host shows it as a
live spectrum and waterfall in the browser, with the receiver's controls next
to it.</p>
<p dir="auto"><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/h0m3us3r/w1700k-sdr/blob/main/docs/images/live-2g4.gif"><img src="https://github.com/h0m3us3r/w1700k-sdr/raw/main/docs/images/live-2g4.gif" alt="The 2.4 GHz band live: spectrum and waterfall" data-animated-image=""></a></p>
<p dir="auto"><em>The whole 2.4 GHz band at 320 Msps, live, with the decimation's roll-off
corrected in the stream ("flatten"): a 262,144-point FFT of every sample, 100
spectra a second (this recording runs at 50 frames a second).</em></p>
<p dir="auto">The MT7996 runs only our own firmware: a ROM patch, WM and WA built from this
repository, with no MediaTek code. It brings the radio up from a cold power-on
by itself, from MediaTek's initialisation and calibration decoded register by
register against a trace of MediaTek's own firmware, and takes its commands
(LO, sample rate, capture, stream, gain, filter) from the host. See
<a href="https://github.com/h0m3us3r/w1700k-sdr/blob/main/docs/architecture.md">docs/architecture.md</a> and <a href="https://github.com/h0m3us3r/w1700k-sdr/blob/main/docs/roadmap.md">docs/roadmap.md</a>.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">What it does</h2><a id="user-content-what-it-does" aria-label="Permalink: What it does" href="#what-it-does"></a></p>
<markdown-accessiblity-table><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Sample rates</td>
<td>40, 80, 160 or 320 Msps of 14-bit complex IQ on every band, independent of the LO and the filter; on band 2 also 640/2 (every other sample of 640 Msps: flat to ±155 MHz, but signals 160–320 MHz away fold in)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Bands</td>
<td>2.4 GHz (MT7976), 5 GHz and 6 GHz (two MT7977s); switching bands is a register write inside a running stream</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Tuning</td>
<td>No channels: the LO is any Hz a band's synthesizer locks at (unit A: 2.081–2.748, 4.647–7.509 and 4.707–7.576 GHz), on its grid of 2.98 Hz (2.4 GHz) or 11.9–14.3 Hz steps; retuned mid-stream (about 1 ms)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Front end</td>
<td>AGC or manual LNA/baseband gain; analog filter 20–320 MHz</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Transport</td>
<td>the radio DMAs into a 1 GiB ring in SoC DRAM; the kernel sends every 8 KiB slice as one UDP datagram, zero-copy, on the 10 GbE port; at 320 Msps half the slices are packed to 28 bits per sample pair on the radio's own cores to fit the link (lossless, automatic)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Host</td>
<td><code>w1700k-stream</code> receives the export and serves the IQ stream to every consumer on the host (shared memory, complex int16, gap-tagged), with an optional correction ("flatten") of the decimation's roll-off applied to the samples themselves; <code>w1700k-spectrum</code>: 262,144-point FFT at 100 frames/s over every sample, browser UI, live controls; <code>sdr-recv</code> records the stream</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></markdown-accessiblity-table>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Screenshots</h2><a id="user-content-screenshots" aria-label="Permalink: Screenshots" href="#screenshots"></a></p>
<p dir="auto"><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/h0m3us3r/w1700k-sdr/blob/main/docs/images/ui-2g4.png"><img src="https://github.com/h0m3us3r/w1700k-sdr/raw/main/docs/images/ui-2g4.png" alt="The web UI at 2.4 GHz"></a></p>
<p dir="auto"><em>2.4 GHz at 320 Msps, flattened: Wi-Fi frames and shorter hopping bursts on a
floor that stays level to the band's edges. The Stream panel shows the link
carrying 9.75 Gbit/s, half of the slices packed, and every sample transformed.</em></p>
<p dir="auto"><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/h0m3us3r/w1700k-sdr/blob/main/docs/images/ui-5g.png"><img src="https://github.com/h0m3us3r/w1700k-sdr/raw/main/docs/images/ui-5g.png" alt="The web UI at 5 GHz"></a></p>
<p dir="auto"><em>Two busy 80 MHz Wi-Fi channels at 5170–5330 MHz in a flattened 320 MHz view.</em></p>
<p dir="auto"><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/h0m3us3r/w1700k-sdr/blob/main/docs/images/ui-zoom.png"><img src="https://github.com/h0m3us3r/w1700k-sdr/raw/main/docs/images/ui-zoom.png" alt="The two channels' edges zoomed to 24 MHz"></a></p>
<p dir="auto"><em>Zoomed to 24 MHz around 5250 MHz: the two channels' edges and the guard gap
between them, in 1.22 kHz bins.</em></p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Repository</h2><a id="user-content-repository" aria-label="Permalink: Repository" href="#repository"></a></p>
<markdown-accessiblity-table><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Path</th>
<th></th>
</tr>
</thead>
