<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Getting started</h2><a id="user-content-getting-started" aria-label="Permalink: Getting started" href="#getting-started"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">You need a W1700K, a 3.3 V USB serial adapter on its console header, an
Ethernet cable to its <code>lan3</code> port for installation, and a 10 GbE NIC on the
host cabled to its <code>wan</code> port (10GBASE-T; an X710 with a copper SFP+ module
works) for the IQ stream.</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="make                                   # everything, from public sources (~10 min plus downloads)
make check                             # the test suite

sudo init/host/link up IFACE           # 192.168.1.10/24 on the NIC cabled to lan3
sudo init/host/tftp-forward enable IFACE
init/install                           # back up the NAND, install, save the radio calibration

sudo init/host/link up IFACE10G 10.77.0.10/24 9216    # the 10 GbE stream link
host/stream/build/w1700k-stream &amp;                      # the stream, for every consumer on this host
host/spectrum/build/w1700k-spectrum --controller &quot;host/bin/w1700k serve&quot; --listen 0.0.0.0:8073"><pre>make                                   <span><span>#</span> everything, from public sources (~10 min plus downloads)</span>
make check                             <span><span>#</span> the test suite</span>

sudo init/host/link up IFACE           <span><span>#</span> 192.168.1.10/24 on the NIC cabled to lan3</span>
sudo init/host/tftp-forward <span>enable</span> IFACE
init/install                           <span><span>#</span> back up the NAND, install, save the radio calibration</span>

sudo init/host/link up IFACE10G 10.77.0.10/24 9216    <span><span>#</span> the 10 GbE stream link</span>
host/stream/build/w1700k-stream <span>&amp;</span>                      <span><span>#</span> the stream, for every consumer on this host</span>
host/spectrum/build/w1700k-spectrum --controller <span><span>"</span>host/bin/w1700k serve<span>"</span></span> --listen 0.0.0.0:8073</pre></div>
<p dir="auto">Then open <code>http://HOST:8073/</code> and press ▶. The first start loads the radio
firmware and brings the radio up (about 2 s). Without the browser, the same
operations are <code>host/bin/w1700k load|start|band|tune|gain|filter|stop|status</code>.</p>
<p dir="auto">Build requirements: a C/C++ compiler, make, git, Python 3.10+, the usual
Buildroot prerequisites, clang, ld.lld, llvm-objcopy and llvm-readobj (RISC-V
firmware), openssl (firmware container encryption), and
<code>libfftw3-dev</code> and <code>zlib</code> for the stream service and the spectrum server. No vendor firmware is
downloaded or needed.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Read before use</h2><a id="user-content-read-before-use" aria-label="Permalink: Read before use" href="#read-before-use"></a></p>
<ul dir="auto">
<li><strong>Our firmware is receive-only.</strong> It runs none of MediaTek's transmit
calibrations and keeps the MAC's transmit path disabled; a gapless watch at
5250 MHz saw nothing during a load, where MediaTek's firmware transmits a
full-power burst. Still, do not cable an antenna port straight into a
sensitive instrument without an attenuator.</li>
<li><strong>Our ROM patch writes no eFuse.</strong> MediaTek's ROM patches since 2025
permanently program one eFuse gate bit on their first run (also with
MediaTek's own driver); a unit that ever ran one has that bit set.</li>
<li><strong>Installation replaces the stock operating system.</strong> <code>init/install</code> first
copies the whole NAND to the host; keep that copy. Restoring stock needs a
raw NAND writer, which this repository does not provide.</li>
<li>Accessing undocumented MT7996 or AN7581 addresses can hang the SoC or wedge
the radio until a power cycle. <code>include/hw/</code> marks every known hazard.</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Licence</h2><a id="user-content-licence" aria-label="Permalink: Licence" href="#licence"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">MIT, except the Linux driver and kernel patches (GPL-2.0-only), U-Boot patches
(GPL-2.0+), the driver's user-space header (GPL-2.0 WITH Linux-syscall-note)
and files derived from MediaTek's mt76 driver (BSD-3-Clause-Clear). See
<a href="https://github.com/h0m3us3r/w1700k-sdr/blob/main/LICENSE">LICENSE</a>.</p>
</article></div></div>]]></description>
