<p id="elk-6a7c749e-c62e-11f1-95db-1d451c967bf7">An ongoing cyberattack has reportedly struck major supermarket chains across Europe, disrupting refrigeration control systems. The attack — which has reportedly seen units set to continuously defrost at 50 degrees Celsius — is also apparently affecting deliveries and in-store pricing powered by electronic shelf labels, <a data-analytics-id="inline-link" href="https://www.ladepeche.fr/2026/10/12/intermarche-leclerc-et-super-u-vises-par-une-cyberattaque-la-temperature-des-frigos-est-montee-jusqua-50-degres-des-degats-considerables-attendus-13596843.php" data-url="https://www.ladepeche.fr/2026/10/12/intermarche-leclerc-et-super-u-vises-par-une-cyberattaque-la-temperature-des-frigos-est-montee-jusqua-50-degres-des-degats-considerables-attendus-13596843.php" target="_blank" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-hl-processed="none" data-article-category="tech-industry" data-mrf-recirculation="inline-link"><em>La Dépêche</em></a><em> </em>reports<em>.</em></p><p>According to the report, "several" major retailers have been targeted by a cyberattack, which has hit Danfoss-made refrigeration controls used by French supermarkets Intermarché, Leclerc, and Coopérative U. Indeed, the latter's president, Dominique Schelcher, has confirmed as of Monday morning that "A cyberattack has been affecting the refrigeration production facilities of several stores in our network since last night." Schelcher said teams had been mobilized to fix the issue and promised an investigation into the incident, which he described as "extremely serious."</p><div data-hydrate="true" id="slice-container-newsletterForm-articleInbodyContent-XvAHPdK6z5YMepiMRpPExL"><section><p>Get Tom's Hardware's best news and in-depth reviews, straight to your inbox.</p></section></div>
</div>




