        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Fable Took My Job (217 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://cendyne.dev/posts/2026-10-11-fable-took-my-jorb.html</link>
            <guid>50053216</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 08:30:33 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://cendyne.dev/posts/2026-10-11-fable-took-my-jorb.html">https://cendyne.dev/posts/2026-10-11-fable-took-my-jorb.html</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50053216">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div id="body"><article><time datetime="2026-10-11T19:00:00.000Z" title="10/11/2026, 2:00:00 PM">Published Today</time> - 8 min read - <a href="https://cendyne.dev/posts/2026-10-11-fable-took-my-jorb.txt" rel="nofollow">Text Only</a><p>Its true. In a manner of speaking. If LLMs had not reached Fable / Mythos tier of <em>marketed</em> intelligence, I'd probably still have a job with the place I used to be at. For a short while.</p><p>It wasn't that Fable was better at my job. As the <em>last</em> engineer, Fable was cheaper than my compensation. Evidently, it gave enough confidence to the last vestiges of leadership that they could coast until M&amp;A.</p><figure><img width="128" height="128" alt="snake-shrug" src="https://cendyne.dev/s/512/cendyne/snake-shrug" sizes="512px"><figcaption></figcaption></figure><p>I've been through layoffs driven by budget cuts. I had to mechanically justify "I need this person because this product I'm oversee is critical to the business and still needs ownership" against a budget ceiling. Those I trusted to build out a greenfield product had their positions eliminated. The status quo was more important to maintain than resolving our operational issues with the next generation of our infrastructure.</p><p>That first meeting crushed my soul. I heard my ears ring more violently than ever. The color drained from the world. I could barely walk straight.</p><p>And, the next day, I went to a house party with two of my reports present knowing that both of them would lose their job next week with zero notice.</p><p>I know first hand what it is like to know and later execute on a layoff.</p><p>Finally, it was my time. I've had grief several times expecting it to come earlier. When it finally came, I took it well for a few hours.</p><figure><img width="128" height="128" alt="my-heart" src="https://cendyne.dev/s/512/cendyne/my-heart" sizes="512px"><figcaption></figcaption></figure><p>Then my heart went to over 100 bpm as the fear and doubt for my future set in. And it didn't ease off for over a week.</p><p>Do you know how hard it is to sleep when all you can hear is your own heartbeat? Do you know how hard it is to function with four effective hours of sleep when you offer your body ten? It is rough.</p><p>Several in my social circles are having difficulty too. Six or Seven in a DevOps interest group have been laid off or have received a layoff notice in the last month. Work at one of FAANG of all places and oops, you're not important now! Here's 6 months of severance, we're so nice. "Ship happens" oh right, we can't ship your stuff because of some material restriction or let you pick up a box with some dignity, so we destroyed your stuff. GLHF.</p><p>Then there's that hiring manager who interviewed me at another company who then got laid off one week later because the CEO made a substantively late "AI is changing how we develop software" article. And another who lost their whole team, despite actively interviewing people in the USA, because a top-down decision was to offshore again after AI assistance raised the capability of work for the less passionate, less experienced, and less costly workforce.</p><p>"AI took my job" is far more fun to say than "AI contributed to my role being eliminated."</p><p>The frontier models are not good enough to replace an experienced engineer. They still are not, even though the marketing by the US frontier labs (and their 'I quit because I'm scared' employees) scream of what's to come.</p><p>These models lack the taste that makes for effective product decision making. It is cheaper than ever for a product manager to say "Someone complains about this on reddit, let's implement it", while the tasteful leader says 'No, that's not what our product is. Making that means we commit to it for a long time and our customers really want <code>{something else we already do, but not perfectly}</code> instead.'</p><p>These models lack the foresight to predict consequences weeks, months, years down the line on decisions and commitments — in operating costs, complexity, accessibility, and affordability to the very customers the product is for. All those secondary things really matter in making a product successful and worth buying. The slop services out there omit these secondary things all the time and you can <em>taste</em> it.</p><p>And yet, models <em>are</em> evidently able to give <a href="https://shopify.engineering/back-to-native" target="_blank">Shopify the confidence</a> to yeet react-native out into the ocean and reach back to native iOS and android apps, entirely machine-coded while overseen by otherwise experienced and tasteful software developers.</p><figure><img width="128" height="128" alt="snake-battery-ocean" src="https://cendyne.dev/s/512/cendyne/snake-battery-ocean" sizes="512px"><figcaption></figcaption></figure><p>And, I agree with Shopify. Models <em>are</em> getting good enough where they can be driven step by step, with a carefully crafted harness, to reproduce and port the behaviors they've A/B tested into oblivion as humans. They can push a rock up a hill. Then up the next hill, and so on — when the technique can be measured and check-pointed.</p><p>Agents are terribly good at copying things across domains. Just look at Theo's "for the memes" <a href="https://github.com/pingdotgg/ts-rust" target="_blank">typescript in rust</a> port. It ported the ts-go code to rust. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ND_kRgU_WlY&amp;t=2577s" target="_blank">According to Theo</a>, Opus 5.5 reproduced bugs from the go version in the rust version exactly. On the other hand, Astra (the rival to Fable), failed at something so familiar, well known, and well documented.</p><p>We're heading towards the end of the year and I still do not see how these copy-machines with "<em>super</em> intelligence" inside are going to replace humans. What they have done is convince those with money to spend differently on their fellow humans.</p><p>Anyway, I got something else now. I get to see screens like this all day:</p><p><img data-blurhash="L03IYJofM{oft7fQayfQWBfQj[fQ" data-width="645" data-height="544" data-ratio="true" src="https://cendyne.dev/c/wT7YTvUP?width=645" alt="hmm hmm hmm hmmm" width="645" height="544"></p><p>The joy of programming is dying. The joy of delivering results lives on.</p><p><img data-blurhash="E02rs+xuRjRjIUWB_3xuWBRjRjfQ" data-width="450" data-height="112" data-ratio="true" src="https://cendyne.dev/c/v1sn6dsV?width=645" alt="I keep hallucinating" width="450" height="112"></p><p><em>I'm starting to think Frontier lab employees are going crazy because they're staring at this all day.</em></p></article></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Zod 4.6.6 compromised by malware? (177 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://github.com/colinhacks/zod/commit/d821685ea6732436fcdf786a81d076958471e467</link>
            <guid>50052814</guid>
