<p>In a <a href="https://pine64.org/2022/01/31/pinephone-community-poll-results/">survey</a> that PINE64 took of 3079 PinePhone users in January 2022, 39.3% selected Phosh as their favorite interface, compared to 15.5% for Sxmo/Sxmo, 15.0% for Plasma Mobile and 6.7% for Lomiri (formerly Unity8). Despite Phosh only starting its development in <a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Purism-Libre-5-Dev-Docs">April 2018</a>, more users preferred it over Lomiri and Plasma Mobile, which have been under development since <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20130306104754/https://wiki.ubuntu.com/MirSpec">March 2013</a> and <a href="https://opensource.com/life/15/7/weekly-news-july-31">July 2015</a>, respectively. Purism’s strategy of using the existing infrastructure and applications from GNOME allowed the tiny company to create a usable interface faster than other competing interfaces.</p>



<p>It is surprising that Phosh became the most popular mobile Linux interface, considering that the Qt toolkit and KDE had such a head start over GTK and GNOME in supporting mobile environments. Qt Quick was introduced with Qt 5.1 in June 2013 to make Qt controls adaptive for small screens, and Qt Mobile was introduced in December 2013 to make them cross-platform, and KDE’s Kirigami library for adaptive applications <a href="https://dot.kde.org/2016/03/30/kde-proudly-presents-kirigami-ui/">was introduced</a> in March 2016. GTK didn’t gain similar abilities until 2019 with libhandy and GNOME didn’t have an answer to Kirigami until libadwaita was <a href="https://blogs.gnome.org/alicem/2021/12/31/libadwaita-1-0/">introduced in 2021</a>.</p>



<p>Phosh has some glaring limitations. Its default Squeekboard keyboard is basic and kludgy. Only in 2025 did Phosh gain <a href="https://phosh.mobi/posts/osk-completion/">an alternative keyboard</a> with word correction and word completion, plus the ability to select from multiple characters by long pressing a key. The desktop mode in Phosh to output video to an external monitor or projector has a very limited interface. Phosh has no camera app, and most installations rely on <a href="https://github.com/kgmt0/megapixels">Megapixels</a> developed by Martijn Braam for the PinePhone or its <a href="https://gitlab.com/tui/millipixels">Millipixels</a> fork developed by Purism for the Librem 5. Millipixels only gained basic support for auto-focus and the ability to shoot video <a href="https://tuxphones.com/purism-librem-5-linux-phone-auto-camera-millipixels-app-photo-video-recording/">in January 2023</a>.</p>



<p>Phosh doesn’t have the three buttons at the bottom or the swipe gestures from Android that most people are used to. Plasma Mobile’s interface is much closer to the familiar Android interface, and it is nicer visually. Phosh’s interface is much simplier. It doesn’t use system navigation buttons and has fewer configuration options than Plasma Mobile. A swipe from the bottom displays Phosh’s <a href="https://phosh.mobi/posts/phosh-overview/">Overview screen</a> where apps can be opened or closed. A swipe from the top displays a <a href="https://phosh.mobi/posts/custom-plugins-dev/">Quick-Setting</a> panel, notifications, and buttons in the top corners to lock or turn off the phone.</p>



