<p>Phosh became the more popular interface, partly because Purism was paying developers to work on it, so it was more stable, whereas Plasma Mobile was more prone to stalls and crashes. Plasma relies on hardware acceleration from the GPU to speed up its interface, which isn’t supported by the drivers for the A64 and i.MX 8M Quad in the PinePhone and Librem 5, whereas Phosh is faster and more responsive just using software rendering. Phosh also requires less RAM, although it does run more background processes that use more CPU.</p>



<h3 id="adoption-of-mobile-interfaces-by-linux-distros"><a></a>Adoption of mobile interfaces by Linux distros</h3>



<p>Phosh succeeded because its close adherence to a common desktop stack made it easier to package and distribute in different distros. Phosh was first adopted by postmarketOS (now Nura) in September 2018, just a two months after Purism first debuted it. The close ties between the Phosh developers and Debian helped get Phosh packaged into Debian in February 2020, where it became the basis of the new Mobian distro. Phosh allowed Debian, Arch, Manjaro, Fedora, OpenSUSE and NixOS to become mobile distros for the first time, which in turn encouraged Linux application developers to start testing their software in a mobile interface to see how it functioned on smaller screens. Because Phosh didn’t stay isolated in one Linux distro, it attracted more volunteers to help develop the software and work on porting it.</p>



<p>Over time, Plasma Mobile has followed Phosh’s example and increasingly unified with its Plasma desktop stack. Originally the Plasma Mobile stack was quite different from the standard desktop Plasma stack, since Plasma Mobile was based on the oFono telephony library and could be run either on the standard Linux kernel or on the <a href="https://halium.org/">Halium Project</a>, which allows Plasma Mobile to run in an LXC container on top of an Android kernel and drivers, and using libhybris to translate its Linux calls to libc into Android’s Bionic. Over time the KDE has jettisoned parts of Plasma Mobile to unify it with its desktop stack. In December 2020, KDE <a href="https://plasma-mobile.org/2020/12/14/plasma-mobile-technical-debt/">announced</a> that it would get rid of Halium and only run on a Linux kernel, because that change would allow Plasma Mobile to employ a traditional Linux userspace without blobs and upgrade their KWin composer. A year later in December 2021, Plasma Mobile <a href="https://plasma-mobile.org/2021/12/07/plasma-mobile-gear-21-12/">announced</a> that it was switching from the oFono library to ModemManager, because it was more stable and better maintained than oFono. Finally, in February 2026, Plasma Mobile <a href="https://www.neowin.net/news/kde-plasma-66-is-finally-here-with-lots-of-new-goodies-to-try-out/">announced</a> it would be switching from the Maliit on-screen keyboard to the new Plasma Keyboard. Over time, KDE has incorporated Kirigami into more of its standard applications, so it doesn’t need two different applications for Plasma and Plasma Mobile.</p>



<p>The unification of the mobile and desktop stacks made it easier for Plasma Mobile to follow Phosh’s example in getting packaged and incorporated into all the major Linux desktop distributions, so they could also be used on mobile devices. Nearly every major distro started testing its code on the PinePhone, which helped get more people testing and contributing to Phosh and Plasma Mobile.</p>



<p>Despite the fact that these two interfaces have fewer developers than desktop GNOME and desktop Plasma, they have don’t need nearly as much manpower because they can piggy back on the desktop stack and the convergent apps, so they can thrive with much less dev work and maintenance than big siloed codebases like Ubuntu Touch, Firefox OS, Tizen, webOS, etc.</p>



<p>Sxmo (Simple X Mobile) has followed simple strategy of building on top an existing deskop stack. It was created by Miles Alan, as a “<a href="https://sxmo.org/docs/user/sxmo.7.html">collection of simple programs and shell scripts</a>” on top of Sway and its wlroots library. It was first released <a href="https://forum.pine64.org/showthread.php?tid=9913">in February 2020</a>, and quickly became the second most popular interface on the PinePhone due to its light-weight and simple design, which made it easy to customize. Sxmo has been incorporated into the mobile distros (postmarketOS, Mobian and DanctNIX), but the desktop distros haven’t adopted it, which has limited its reach.</p>



<p>UBport’s Lomiri interface is faster and more responsive and consumes less RAM than Phosh and Plasma Mobile, but it was originally isolated in Ubuntu Touch. Canonical spent years developing its Unity8 interface, which is based on Qt Quick and used in Ubuntu Touch. The UBports community <a href="https://9to5linux.com/ubports-unity8-becomes-lomiri-the-linux-environment-for-ubuntu-touch">renamed the interface as Lomiri</a> in February 2020. UBports has followed a similar strategy of trying to attract more people to its Lomiri interface by getting it incorporated into the major distros. UBports modified Ubuntu Touch and the Lomiri interface so they could run on either Halium or a standard Linux kernel, and switched to using more standardized libraries that were easier to upgrade over time. Nonetheless its small number of volunteers has struggled to maintain its huge codebase, and update it to new versions of Qt.</p>



<p>Despite the head start of Plasma Mobile and Lomiri, they stayed isolated for longer in KDE Neon and Ubuntu Touch. In contrast, Phosh got adopted almost immediately by postmarketOS, and once it got packaged by Debian in early 2020, most of the other major distros quickly adopted it. When postmarketOS was first released in May 2017, it used Weston, which is Wayland’s reference compositor, but within the <a href="https://nura.eco/blog/2017/09/03/100-days-of-postmarketos/">first 100 days</a> of its existence, postmarketOS adopted Plasma Mobile. However, postmarketOS chose to ship its PinePhone Community Edition with Phosh due to its greater stability.</p>



