<p>Unlike in the past, when mobile Linux interfaces were isolated in a few distros, many distros now support Phosh, Plasma Mobile and Lomiri. Because mobile Linux is no longer isolated and locked into silos controlled by large companies, such as Nokia, Samsung, LG, Mozilla and Canonical, it has a much greater chance of succeeding and attracted contributors than it did in the past. Because Phosh and Plasma Mobile and Sxmo are based on using existing software stacks that are maintained by outside organizations, it is much less likely to be abandoned, as happened with Firefox OS, or to ossify and fade into obscurity, as happened with Hildon.</p>



<h3 id="pine64-s-attempt-to-get-around-the-cost-of-developing-software"><a></a><strong>PINE64’s</strong> <strong>attempt to get around the cost of developing software</strong></h3>



<p>PINE64 was founded as a crowd-funding campaign in October 2015 by TL Lim and Johnson Jeng to develop a single board computer based on the Allwinner A64 processor. The company based in Hong Kong focuses on producing economical Linux hardware devices. It gets around the high costs of developing software by just producing the hardware and then invites the community to develop the software for its devices. By allying with various different Linux distros, PINE64 initially had a great deal of success.</p>



<p>PINE64 probably shipped <a href="https://forums.puri.sm/t/rob-braxmans-review-of-the-librem-5/21203/146?page=8">over 100,000 PinePhones</a>, which greatly stimulated the development of mobile Linux. The release of the PinePhone led to the creation of the Mobian (<strong>Mob</strong>ile Deb<strong>ian</strong>) and DanctNIX (based on Arch ARM) distros and inspired the creation of the Sxmo interface. The PinePhone helped Plasma Mobile decide that it only needed to run on a Linux stack and pushed UBports to add support for running on a Linux kernel. It also caused many of the major distros to work on porting to a mobile device for the first time.</p>



<p>PINE64 played an important service for the community by getting cheap hardware into people’s hands so they develop software for that hardware, but it made the fundamental mistake of focusing on the distros, which only works if the hardware already has kernel support and good free/open source drivers. Drew DeVault, the lead developer of Sway and one of the volunteer developers who worked on the PinePhone, <a href="https://drewdevault.com/blog/Pine64s-weird-priorities/">observed</a> that PINE64 was putting the cart before the horse, by helping to finance the packaging work in particular distos, rather than first paying developers to work on the booting and driver development, that would have benefitted all the distros.</p>



<p>Because PINE64 chose hardware for the PinePhone that was mostly already supported by FOSS drivers, it was able to attract a large number of distros to work on the phone. Volunteer developer Ondřej Jirman (aka “mega” or “megi”) created a <a href="https://xnux.eu/howtos/build-pinephone-kernel.html">custom Linux kernel</a> for the PinePhone. Volunteers at UBports did the initial work on bringing up the hardware. Arnaud Ferraris (aka “a-wai”) at Mobian developed the telephony stack using the <a href="https://gitlab.com/mobian1/eg25-manager" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><u>eg25-manager</u></a> in userspace. Martijn Braam at postmarketOS worked on the camera. PINE64 initially released a series of five development batches of the PinePhone, which had different distros preinstalled (Ubuntu Touch, postmarketOS with Phosh, Manjaro with Phosh, Manjaro with Plasma Mobile and Mobian with Phosh). For each phone sold, PINE64 donated $10 to the distro which provided the software. After that, PINE64 <a href="https://pine64.org/2020/05/15/may-update-pinetab-pre-orders-pinephone-qi-charging-more/">planned</a> to let the buyer select which distro to support by selecting the distro’s logo to display on the back plate of the PinePhone’s case.</p>



<p>Nonetheless, there were arguments among the different distros behind the scenes over the design of the hardware projects. PINE64’s founder, TL Lim, grew frustrated and decided to just ship PINE64’s devices with one distro and one interface preinstalled. Given the complexity of trying to work with so many different distros, this wasn’t an unreasonable decision, but it had to be handled with care to not alienate the other distros. Unfortunately, Lim chose to only ship PINE64 devices with Manjaro and Plasma Mobile preinstalled. PINE64’s own <a href="https://pine64.org/2022/01/31/pinephone-community-poll-results/">poll of PinePhone users</a> showed that Phosh was the most popular interface, but it was controlled by Purism, a competitor to PINE64. Plasma Mobile wasn’t a bad choice, since it was a community-based interface that needed corporate support. Manjaro, however, was a very poor choice, since it wasn’t doing any software dev work and has been widely criticized for its bad practice of shipping software in development that is known to be broken, which creates more work and public backlash for the projects developing the software.</p>



<p>Many of the crucial volunteers who worked on the PinePhone were burned out by the time that development on the PinePhone Pro started. They were frustrated by the internal arguments and the fact that PINE64 had shifted from sharing revenue with many distros to only paying Manjaro, rather than the people who were doing the necessary dev work. The PinePhone Pro’s hardware required quite a bit of software dev work to support it. In August 2022, Martijn Braam at postmarketOS <a href="https://blog.brixit.nl/why-i-left-pine64/">publicly broke up</a> with PINE64 due to its bad decisions in favor of Manjaro. PINE64’s <a href="https://pine64.org/2022/08/18/a-response-to-martijns-blog/">response</a> failed to address Braam’s frustrations, and Drew DeVault posted that “<a href="https://drewdevault.com/blog/PINE64-let-us-down/">PINE64 has let its community down</a>.” Most of the volunteer developers stopped collaborating with PINE64, so the community-based company didn’t have the manpower to do the necessary software development for its new hardware. Most distros weren’t willing to work on a phone that couldn’t even properly boot. Thousands of people bought the PinePhone Pro after it was released in December 2021, expecting that eventually it would become a functional device, but it still had major software problems when its sales were cancelled in August 2025. The failure of the PinePhone Pro illustrates that PINE64 had its priorities backwards by focusing on one distro that mainly does packaging work, rather than focusing on the booting and kernel work.</p>



<p>Another of the problems was that PINE64 sent out its free hardware samples to the same small number of volunteers. By the time that the PinePhone Pro started its development, those volunteers were overwhelmed by all the new hardware that PINE64 was releasing, which all needed serious software dev work to ever be functional. With so many projects needing attention, the volunteers couldn’t focus their energy on any one device, so all of them got neglected.</p>



<p>PINE64 charges such low prices for its hardware, that it doesn’t have the necessary funds to pay for serious software dev work. However, its revenue sharing model with the distros was working to convince talented people associated with those distros to do the hard work of supporting its hardware. Then, PINE64 undermined its whole community development model by deciding that it would only sell its hardware with one distro and one interface preinstalled, and its decision to only share its revenue with that one distro. Then, PINE64 made matters worse by only listening to Manjaro in how it designed its hardware, which alienated many of the volunteers from other distros that it needed to do the software dev work. Once the best volunteers abandoned PINE64, the company hasn’t been able to complete the development of the PinePhone Pro, PineNote, PineTab2 and many of its other projects, so the people who ordered these devices are unlikely to ever be able to use them as fully functional devices.</p>



<p>Rather than sharing revenue with many different distros, PINE64 should have used any profits that it generated to pay developers to work on the drivers and kernel support for the hardware. It could have taken a community-based approach by observing who was making progress, and helping to finance their work. Because the Allwinner A64 already had decent free/open source drivers, volunteers were able to fill in the gaps with the PinePhone. However, many of the kernel changes that they made never got upstreamed to mainline Linux, which PINE64 could have required if it were paying for that work.</p>



<p>PINE64 also could have directed its revenue sharing program to help finance development work that would benefit all users of PinePhone, regardless of which distro they chose, such as Martijn Braam’s <a href="https://blog.brixit.nl/megapixels-2-0/">work</a> on the <a href="https://git.sr.ht/~martijnbraam/megapixels">Megapixels</a> camera app or <a href="https://liliputing.com/open-source-pinephone-modem-firmware-now-supports-audio-gps-and-power-management/">Biktorgj’s work</a> on freeing the <a href="https://github.com/the-modem-distro/pinephone_modem_sdk">EG25-G modem firmware</a>. Given that Phosh, Plasma Mobile, Lomiri and <a href="https://sxmo.org/">Sxmo/Swmo</a> were being packaged in many different distros, it would have been better to pay people to work on developing those mobile interfaces than giving money to the distros. There are plenty of volunteers in the community who were willing to work on porting distros to the PinePhone, but there are far fewer people who are willing to do difficult driver and software dev work in their spare time.</p>



<h3 id="the-waning-enthusiasm-for-linux-phones"><a></a>The waning enthusiasm for Linux phones</h3>



<p>After the initial enthusiasm for the Librem 5, PinePhone and PinePhone Pro in 2020-21, there was a noticeable drop in people’s interest in Linux phones. The number of posts about the Librem 5 and PinePhone on Reddit and the Purism and PINE64 forums reduced in 2022-25. The subsequent announcements of new Linux phones, namely the F(x)tec Pro<sup>1</sup> X with Sailfish OS and Ubuntu Touch, the Volla phones with Ubuntu Touch, Furi Labs FLX1/s/s+, the Jolla C2 with Sailfish OS, Liberux NEXX, Commodore Callback 8020 and Mecha Comet, haven’t generated nearly as much public interest and community engagement as the announcements of the Librem 5, PinePhone and PinePhone Pro. This difference is partly due to the fact that Purism and PINE64 were established companies that were already shipping Linux devices and they had community forums and Subreddits where people could gather and discuss the upcoming devices.</p>



<p>Nonetheless, I suspect that many of the people who bought the Librem 5 and PinePhone had a similar experiences to mine which dampened their enthusiasm for Linux phones over time. When both of those phones first arrived, they were fascinating devices for geeks like me, who had been playing around with Linux for years on their desktop PCs. I had a blast flashing different distros on the PinePhone and I frequently downloaded the latest version of PureOS and reflashed my Librem 5. I ran benchmarks on the two phones and eagerly followed the dev work to add support for the phones’ hardware. I had spent years waiting for the Librem 5, and after it arrived, I followed the development of Phosh and Adwaita by the Purism developers, like I was rooting for my favorite sports team. I considered the development of Phosh and Plasma Mobile to be the most exciting thing that was happening in the Linux world, and I enjoyed trying out new versions of the interfaces to see what was new in each release.</p>



<p>The first year after the Librem 5 and PinePhone were released were exciting times. I was so intrigued by the phones that I looked up every <a href="https://source.puri.sm/Librem5/docs/community-wiki/-/wikis/hardware/librem-5-component-list">component on the Librem 5</a> and <a href="https://wiki.pine64.org/index.php?title=PinePhone_component_list">PinePhone</a> schematics and documented them on the projects’ wikis. However, each time I flashed a new distro on the PinePhone to try it out, I only played with the phone for a day or two, before it went back in the drawer on my desk. I took the PinePhone apart and admired how it was designed, but I never used the phone for very long before I put it down. Its performance was poor. It took a long time to load apps and there was a noticeable delay in the interface. I also found it distracting to use the phone when the screen was dimmed or the battery was low, because of the noticeable flicker on the screen. I considered pulling out the soldering iron to <a href="https://izzo.pro/pinephone-vccq-mod/">implement a fix</a> that would speed up the Flash memory storage, but I feared that I might accidentally ruin the board. I expected that PINE64 would issue an new version of the PinePhone that would fix the problems, but instead PINE64 dismissed the screen flicker as a problem and hasn’t changed the eMMC’s voltage from 3.3 to 1.8 volts, which would have increased its sequential read speed from <a href="https://amosbbatto.wordpress.com/2021/12/10/comparing-l5-and-pp/#performance">55 to 125 MB/s</a>. I was also annoyed that PINE64 provided no way to communicate with its hardware developers, so I wasn’t able to get <a href="https://amosbbatto.wordpress.com/2021/12/10/comparing-l5-and-pp/#tech-support-and-community">errors fixed</a> that I found in the PinePhone schematics.</p>



<p>Unlike the Pinephone which I rarely used, I kept using the Librem 5, because its better CPU and memory performance made its interface much more responsive than the PinePhone. Nonetheless, the Librem 5’s screen and aluminum frame got hot, since Purism used thermal paste to join the i.MX 8M Quad to the LCD’s backplate to dissipate the processor’s heat through the screen and aluminum frame. I often felt like I was walking around with a heater in my pocket. The battery drained fast, so I was constantly having to charge the Librem 5.</p>



<p>Eventually the Purism developers got the Librem 5’s camera to work, and I tried out each new version of the Millipixels app. It was fun playing with the camera and I learned a lot about how cameras work trying to adjust the settings to get a good photo. However, the Librem 5 took a long time to process each photo in software after taking a picture, since its i.MX 8M Quad processor lacked an image signal processor and hardware encoder in the VPU. It also took me way too long fiddling with the Millipixels’ settings for me to rely on the camera. Whenever I wanted to make sure that I would get a decent photo, I pulled out my Xiaomi Redmi Note 7 running LineageOS to take the photo. Eventually it was possible to shoot video on the Librem 5 at a low resolution and frame rate, but it took so long to process the video and the quality was so limited, that I never bothered using it.</p>



<p>In the end, the PinePhone sat most of the time in a drawer in my desk, and the Librem 5 sat charging on my desk. They were good entertainment for the geek in me, but I didn’t use either of them as my primary phone. Many of the early adopters appear to have had similar experiences with their Linux phones. It was hard to justify buying another Linux phone, when there was already one sitting unused in the drawer. Many people were unwilling to take a chance on another Linux phone after witnessing how the community developers abandoned PINE64 in 2022, and software development stalled for the PinePhone Pro and how Purism refused to refund cancelled orders for the Librem 5.</p>



<p>When Furi Labs <a href="https://www.theregister.com/on-prem/2024/06/26/furi-phone-flx1-debian-smartphone-debuts/674095">announced</a> its upcoming FLX1 in <a href="https://furilabs.com/timeline/">June 2024</a>, it solved many of the problems with the Librem 5 and PinePhone. It contained a 6nm MediaTek Dimensity 900, which was powerful enough shoot video and run 3D games <a href="https://furilabs.com/blog/vulkan-cross-platform-3d-graphics/">with Vulkan</a>. It offers three hardware privacy switches and multi-booting to run multiple operating systems on the phone. The phone’s software was developed by Bardia Moshiri, who is the lead developer of <a href="https://droidian.org/">Droidian</a>, which runs Phosh in a Debian-based distro on top of Halium with an Android kernel. It was based on well-supported projects and software which had already been developed, so its costs would be low, but it had an experienced developer to provide software support. In other words, it would avoid the high costs that troubled the Librem 5 and the lack of software support that bedeviled the PinePhone Pro. People report that the FLX1 and its following FLX1s and FLX1s+ variants work well, but the Linux phone is not well-known and appears to have gotten a limited number of orders. The company’s <a href="https://forum.droidian.org/">forum</a> has been frozen and there has been limited activity on the <a href="https://forum.droidian.org/">Droidian forum</a>, indicating that the phone has gained little traction in the Linux community.</p>



<figure><a href="https://amosbbatto.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/divinedcircuitboard.jpg"><img data-attachment-id="9939" data-permalink="https://amosbbatto.wordpress.com/2026/10/05/linux-phones-alternative/divinedcircuitboard/" data-orig-file="https://amosbbatto.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/divinedcircuitboard.jpg" data-orig-size="720,960" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-title="DivineDCircuitBoard" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-large-file="https://amosbbatto.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/divinedcircuitboard.jpg?w=625" loading="lazy" width="720" height="960" src="https://amosbbatto.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/divinedcircuitboard.jpg?w=720" alt="" srcset="https://amosbbatto.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/divinedcircuitboard.jpg 720w, https://amosbbatto.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/divinedcircuitboard.jpg?w=113 113w, https://amosbbatto.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/divinedcircuitboard.jpg?w=225 225w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 720px) 100vw, 720px"></a></figure>



<p><em>Revision 1.1 of the Divine D. circuit board arrived on <a href="https://mastodon.social/@dawndrums/117071907601926129">Aug 10, 2026</a>.</em></p>



<p>In <a href="https://fosstodon.org/@linmob/113860953049449923">January 2025</a>, Liberux, a startup based in Madrid, Spain, announced its upcoming <a href="https://liberux.net/">NEXX</a> Linux phone based on the RK3588S with specs to make nerds salivate: 4×2.4GHz Cortex-A76, 4x Cortex-A55, Mali-G610 MP4 GPU, 32GB LPDDR4X RAM, 256GB eMMC, 5G modem and 3 hardware kill switches. In June 2025, Liberux started the <a href="https://www.indiegogo.com/en/projects/liberuxlabssl/liberux-nexx--3">crowdfunding</a> for the phone, and a couple days later <a href="https://www.indiegogo.com/en/projects/liberuxlabssl/liberux-nexx--3/updates/3">announced</a> a modular and open architecture that would allow the RAM board with an FPC connector to be upgraded up to 32GB, the eMMC board with a standard socket to be upgraded up to 512GB, and the cellular modem with a custom adapter board to be replaced/upgraded. The price varied between €790 and €1310, depending on the selected configuration. In addition, the NEXX would have two USB-C 3.1 ports to facilitate the phone being used a convergent desktop PC and an AW8737SCSR amplifier and a 3.5mm audio jack for better analog audio.</p>



<p>The NEXX was a revolutionary design, promising to be the first phone in history with two USB ports and the first one with the RAM and eMMC on separate boards, so it could be upgraded and connected like a normal PC. Liberux was attempting to offer the first truly convergent smartphone that had the right software and hardware to replace a desktop PC, yet it hardly generated any headlines in the tech press. It only got <a href="https://liliputing.com/liberux-isnt-giving-up-on-its-ambitious-linux-phone-despite-crowdfunding-fail/">120 pre-orders</a> in a month, which generated a tenth of the $1.7 million in crowdfunding that it needed. When its crowdfunding campaign failed, Liberux <a href="https://www.indiegogo.com/en/projects/liberuxlabssl/liberux-nexx--3/updates/7">reported</a> that it was negotiating with investors for raise the funds to build a prototype. However, its <a href="https://mastodon.social/@Liberux/115615514738875819">last post</a> on the company’s Mastadon account was in November 2025 about “moving forward with new self-funding.” After 11 months of silence, it is looking increasingly doubtful that the NEXX will ever come to market.</p>



<p>There is more hope for the <a href="https://www.hackster.io/sadok-bdiri/divine-d-a-high-performance-gnu-linux-smartphone-be18c7">Dawndrums Divine D.</a> phone, which is being produced by a startup in Tunisia. The startup has continued to make slow progress according to <a href="https://mastodon.social/@dawndrums">its Mastodon posts</a>. They are now on the <a href="https://mastodon.social/@dawndrums/117071907601926129">second revision</a> of their main board and have produced a <a href="https://mastodon.social/@dawndrums/117082333191617418">breakout board</a> for debugging it. They are developing their own distro known as <a href="https://github.com/dawndrums#dawnos">DawnOS</a>, which is based on Mobian and planning to support both the Phosh and Plasma Mobile interfaces. They are <a href="https://liberapay.com/dawndrums/donate">asking for donations</a>, but appear to be largely self funded at this point. Like the NEXX, the Divine D. also promises to be an outstanding convergent phone, supporting up to 32GB RAM and offering a separate HDMI port for video out. It also offers a LoRa (long-range radio) chip, and a pogo pin interface to add accessories to the phone.</p>



<h3 id="jolla-shows-that-there-is-still-demand-for-linux-phones"><a></a>Jolla shows that there is still demand for Linux phones</h3>



<p>While the failed crowdfunding of the Liberux NEXX and the limited sales of the Furi Labs FLX1 would suggest that there isn’t much hope for Linux phones, it is important to keep in mind that these phones were poorly advertised and produced by unknown startups that had no prior track record, so people were reluctant to order them. Bitter experience with the Librem 5 refunds, the failures of the PinePhone Pro, and all the cancelled Linux mobile devices over the years, such as the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ubuntu_Edge">Ubuntu Edge</a> in 2013, the <a href="https://blog.mozilla.org/press/2015/03/firefox-os-proves-flexibility-of-web-ecosystem-expands-with-more-partners-device-categories-and-regions-in-2015/">Firefox OS phones</a> in 2015, the <a href="https://blog.jolla.com/jolla-tablet-closure/">Jolla Tablet</a> in 2015 and the <a href="https://liliputing.com/necunos-nc_1-is-an-expensive-open-source-phone-shaped-thing/">Necunos NC_1</a> in 2019, has taught Linux enthusiasts to be cautious about crowdfunding mobile devices.</p>



<p>However, there are reasons to believe that there is quite a bit of pent-up demand for mobile Linux, and it only requires the right kind of promotion and a company with a credible track record to unlock it. Ironically, the company demonstrating that people still want to use Linux phones is tiny <a href="https://jolla.com/">Jolla</a>, which has somehow managed to survive over the years.</p>



<p>The Finnish engineers who used to work on Nokia’s MeeGo founded their own company named Jolla Oy in 2011 to keep developing that code as Sailfish OS. They took the FOSS parts of MeeGo and added their proprietary <a href="https://sailfishos.org/develop/docs/silica/">Silica</a> interface based on <a href="https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qtqml-index.html">Qt QML</a>, which imitates the <a href="https://wapreview.com/15554/">Swipe UI</a> that Nokia had developed for <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20110622085758/https://wiki.meego.com/ARM/N900">Meego on the N900/N9</a>. They also developed their own proprietary AlienDalvik to run Android apps inside Linux. Jolla deserves credit for developing <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Libhybris">libhybris</a> and helping to maintain much of the code developed by Nokia, including the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OFono">oFono</a> telephony library and the <a href="https://maliit.github.io/">Maliit</a> virtual keyboard. Remarkably, the small Finnish company managed to survive nearly going bankrupt in late 2015, when it cancelled its crowdfunded <a href="https://www.indiegogo.com/en/projects/jolla/jolla-tablet-world-s-first-crowdsourced-tablet">Jolla Tablet</a>, which caused many of its customers who preordered to lose their money. It split off its hardware operations into a separate company named <a href="https://buy.jolla-devices.com/">Jolla-Devices</a>, which sells now Sony smartphones with Sailfish OS preinstalled to the European market, plus Volla Phones with Ubuntu Touch and Pixels with GrapheneOS.</p>



<p>After killing off the original company in 2024, because it was majority owned by Russian investors, who were politically toxic due to the war in Ukraine, Jolla reemerged under a <a href="https://forum.sailfishos.org/t/jolla-2-0-jolla-s-former-management-purchased-jolla-s-business-ip-and-software-assets-from-jolla-oy/17487">new company</a> named JollaBoys that bought the old company’s assets from Jolla Oy. The new Jolla company has returned to the business of selling hardware. Jolla announced its <a href="https://commerce.jolla.com/products/jolla-community-phone">Jolla C2 Community Phone</a> in <a href="https://maemo-freak.com/jolla/c2/">May 2024</a>, and started delivering them <a href="https://forum.sailfishos.org/t/jolla-c2-community-phone-deliveries/19468?page=9">two months later</a>, which helped reestablish the credibility of the company. When Jolla started <a href="https://commerce.jolla.com/products/jolla-phone-preorder">taking preorders</a> for its new <a href="https://commerce.jolla.com/products/jolla-phone-october-2026">Jolla Phone</a> on December 5, 2025, it sold out its crowdfunding goal of 2000 phones within the first 48 hours and <a href="https://jolla.com/content/uploads/2025/12/Jolla-pre-order-campaign-sold-organically-5000-phones-in-a-week.pdf?x26973">5000 phones</a> within the first week. People were pre-ordering the Jolla Phone without having seen any reviews of the phone and not even knowing what processor it would contain. The phone, which initially cost $499 and now costs was €679, was marketed as containing 12GB RAM, 256GB storage, a user-replaceable battery and a physical privacy switch. Jolla’s advertising for the phone clearly struck a chord with European consumers, which proclaimed it to be a “Do It Together (DIT) Linux phone, shaped by the people who use it.” The Finnish company emphasized the independence of the phone from the rest of the mobile phone industry, stating on the order page: “The Jolla Phone is not based on Big Tech technology. It is governed by European privacy thinking and a community-led model.”</p>



<p>Jolla has sold 15,000 units of its phone as of August 2026 just by taking pre-orders for production batches from its web site, and it probably could have sold far more units if it had paid for advertising, expanded beyond the European market, and kept the phone in stock, so customers didn’t have to wait months for the next production batch. Clearly, there is an untapped market of people who are dissatisfied with the reigning Android/iOS duopoly.</p>



<h3 id="the-mobile-industry-is-shutting-down-the-aosp-alternative"><a></a>The mobile industry is shutting down the AOSP alternative</h3>



<p>Today the use of Linux is growing on the desktop. Many people are installing Linux on their PCs, because they have become disgusted by how Microsoft has turned Windows into an advertising platform for surveillance capitalism and a vehicle for AI slop. Rather than buying new PCs, a growing number of people have decided to install Linux, because support for Windows 10 has ended and they can’t upgrade their old PCs to Windows 11 due to Microsoft’s arbitrary TPM restrictions.</p>



<p>Likewise, many people are equally disenchanted with Android and iOS on their phones. More people have grown distrustful of the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surveillance_capitalism">surveillance capitalism</a> which undergirds the Android market and the restrictions and rising prices of Apple’s “walled garden” on the iPhone, yet the options to escape the smartphone duopoly keep shrinking. In the past, people who wanted to gain some control over their digital lives could unlock the bootloader on their phone and install an AOSP derivative such as LineageOS, /e/OS, CalyxOS or GrapheneOS, however the mobile phone industry is increasingly shutting off this escape valve.</p>



<p>Every year there are fewer Android phone models on the market which allow the bootloader to be unlocked. The rise of Chinese brands, which now control roughly 60% of the global smartphone market, has driven many of the traditional phones brands out of the market which did allow their bootloaders to be unlocked. Many of the phone brands that were mod-friendly, such as LG, BQ, LeEco, WileyFox, Essential and Razer, are now gone from the market, and other mod-friendly brands like Sony and HTC now have so few sales that they are no longer relevant in the global smartphone market. Huawei <a href="https://consumer.huawei.com/en/community/details/topicId-133598/">stopped allowing</a> its bootloaders to be unlocked in 2018. In the last couple years, Samsung, ASUS and Realme all followed Huawei’s bad example and no longer allow their bootloaders to be unlocked. Xiaomi, which rose to fame as a mod-friendly brand, has recently implemented so <a href="https://new.c.mi.com/uk/post/43834">many restrictions</a> to <a href="https://github.com/zenfyrdev/bootloader-unlock-wall-of-shame/blob/main/brands/xiaomi/README.md">obtain</a> its <a href="https://github.com/realdtn2/xiaomi-unlocking-rooting-guide">unlock codes</a>, that it is unlikely that modders will bother porting custom ROMs to future Xiaomi models.</p>



<p>As noted in my <a href="https://amosbbatto.wordpress.com/2025/11/02/lineageos-statistics/">article on LineageOS statistics</a>, the brands which have good policies for bootloader unlocking has shrunk from 42.5% to about 7% of the global smartphone market over the last couple years. The brands which allow their bootloaders to be unlocked (without major restrictions like Xiaomi) only sold about 87 million phones in 2025, including Lenovo/Motorola (<a href="https://www.linkedin.com/posts/runar-bjorhovde_2025-was-a-remarkable-year-for-motorola-mobility-activity-7434224139273519105-od8E">60M</a>), Google (<a href="https://www.androidcentral.com/phones/google-pixel/google-reportedly-plans-to-exit-china-in-2027-produce-pixel-line-in-india-vietnam">12M</a>), OnePlus (<a href="https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/international/business/oneplus-was-the-ultimate-flagship-killer-now-its-fighting-for-relevance/articleshow/132435919.cms?from=mdr">11.6M</a>), Nothing (<a href="https://9to5google.com/2026/07/24/nothing-exiting-global-markets-report/">2M</a>), GigaSet (<a href="https://www.gigaset.com/hq_en/cms/home/press/news-detail/news/detail/News/gigaset-back-on-track-mitel-relocates-phone-production-to-bocholt.html">&gt;1M</a>), Sony (<a href="https://www.youtube.com/shorts/AeTqOV_7EVI">400k</a>), Fairphone (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairphone">145k</a>), SHIFT (<a href="https://growjo.com/company/SHIFT">10k</a>), Teracube, Volla and Brax. Together those brands only represent 7% of the <a href="https://community.oneplus.com/thread/2050300550242631684">1245.5 million</a> total smartphones sold in 2025.</p>



<p>However, that is an overestimate, since most phone models never get good custom ROMs made for them. A good portion of Motorola and Google phones are sold through carriers, and many carriers like Verizon don’t allow their phones to ever be unlocked, even if they are no longer under contract. MediaTek Dimensity and UNISOC Tiger processors have gained market share in recent years, and those chipmakers don’t publish the source code for their kernels like Qualcomm does for its Snapdragon processors. A few brands that use MediaTek SoC’s, such as Motorola, Xiaomi, Volla, Gigaset, TeraCube and Brax, do publish their kernel code, but that code for many other MediaTek phones will never get published, nor will custom ROMs be made for them. UNISOC processors are basically black boxes for the modding community. The bootloaders of all phones with Apple A-series, Huawei Kirin and recent Samsung Exynos processors are locked, so the modding community is restricted to just Qualcomm Snapdragon, Google Tensor and some MediaTek Dimensity processors. Motorola and Xiaomi have switched most of their budget model lines from Snapdragon to MediaTek Dimensity processors, so they are harder to mod.</p>



<p>Aside from the Motorola Moto G05, which was released in Jan 2025 and replaced 8 months later by the G06, there has not been a single budget phone released by a major phone maker in 2025 or 2026 that has gained official support by LineageOS. Gaining official LineageOS support is important, because many of the other custom ROMs are based on LineageOS and only port to its officially supported models. Just looking at phone models with official LineageOS support which allow their bootloaders to be unlocked, only about 20 million or 2% of smarphones sold each year are good for installing custom ROMs.</p>



<div><p>The situation is about to get much worse. Sadly, Oppo just <a href="https://www.androidauthority.com/oneplus-north-america-europe-exit-plans-reasons-future-3687095/">announced</a> that it will pull its OnePlus brand from the North American and European markets. OnePlus phones will still be sold in India and China (where the bootloader can be unlocked if the user presents an official Chinese ID card), but OnePlus is essentially gone for the modding community. Nothing, which was a rising star in the modding world, has been losing sales. The London-based company <a href="https://www.androidauthority.com/nothing-market-exits-layoffs-rumor-3691156/">denies rumors</a> that it will pull out of a third of the countries where it currently sells phones, but confirms that it plans to cut 100 jobs, which comprise 12% of its workforce. At any rate, none of the phones which Nothing has released in the last two years have gained official LineageOS support.</p><p>Samsung has been making it more and more difficult to mod its phones over the last decade, so the Korean company’s decision to <a href="https://www.androidauthority.com/samsung-bootloader-unlocking-disabled-one-ui-8-3581366/">lock the bootloader</a> for all its phones getting its ONE UI 8 interface wasn’t a surprise, but Xiaomi’s decision to start <a href="https://www.androidpolice.com/xiaomi-bootloader-unlock-restricted-to-one-device-per-user-year/">restricting its bootloader unlock codes</a> was a major blow for the modding community. The only major global brand left for modding is Motorola, but there are only unofficial ports available for recent Motorola models, meaning that some things don’t work correctly or security patches aren’t being provided. Motorola promises to <a href="https://www.cnet.com/tech/services-and-software/privacy-focused-grapheneos-is-coming-to-motorola-phones-in-2027/">launch phones with GrapheneOS</a> in 2027, but they will be <a href="https://www.androidauthority.com/grapheneos-motorola-phones-pricing-3701347/">flagship models</a> which probably cost over $1000 and be limited to a few select countries.</p></div>



<p>Aside from the Motorola Moto G15 (released in December 2024), people are unlikely to find a new phone where they can install an official LineageOS port for sale in Africa, most of Latin America (except Mexico) and most of Asia (except India, Japan, Taiwan, Singapore and Malaysia). The only option to run a custom ROM in most of the world is to buy a used Motorola or Xiaomi phone from a couple years ago, such as the Moto G (2024), Moto G32, Moto G34, Moto G84, POCO F5, POCO F5 Pro, POCO F6 Pro or Redmi Note 13 Pro. There are more recent phones like the <a href="https://xdaforums.com/t/rom-16-unofficial-roadstr-lineageos-23-2-unofficial-builds-for-moto-edge-70-x70-air.4796905/">Moto Edge 70</a>, which have unofficial LineageOS ports, but it is a gamble if they will ever be officially supported by LineageOS.</p>



<p>As Google has pushed phone makers to support their phones for longer, many companies have decided to collect more personal data from their users for better targeted advertising and for training artificial intelligences. The smartphone industry is moving away from a business model based on planned obsolescence toward one based on surveillance capitalism, and the phone makers don’t want their users to opt out of the system, so they have removed the option to unlock the bootloader and made it harder to gain root access. That way they can recoup the cost of providing more years of support for their phones by monetizing their users’ data.</p>



<p>In addition, Google has made it harder to maintain custom ROMs, because it has moved more and more <a href="https://www.howtogeek.com/your-android-phone-isnt-as-open-source-as-you-think/">functionality from AOSP</a> into its proprietary libraries and services (starting with Android 4.1 in 2012), and largely <a href="https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2018/07/googles-iron-grip-on-android-controlling-open-source-by-any-means-necessary/">abandoned the development</a> of many of the free/open source apps in AOSP in favor of its proprietary apps. Starting with AOSP 16 in 2025, Google <a href="https://www.androidauthority.com/google-not-killing-aosp-3566882/">stopped including</a> the device trees and driver binaries for Pixel devices in AOSP, so ROM developers no longer had target devices, where they could easily test out and adapt the latest versions of AOSP on running hardware. Now custom ROM developers have to do painful trial and error to make the device trees for Pixel phones just like all the other phone brands. Google also squashed the kernel’s commit history, which makes it very difficult for ROMs like GrapheneOS to create their own custom kernels, because they can’t easily take new features, bug fixes and security patches from Google’s kernels.</p>



<p>Given how the smartphone industry is locking down their hardware and pushing business models that are inimical to users’ privacy, there is increased opportunity for Linux phones to be marketed to people who want to escape the Android/iOS duopoly and surveillance capitalism.</p>



<h3 id="three-types-of-linux-phones-are-now-possible"><a></a><strong>Three</strong> <strong>types</strong> <strong>of Linux phones are now possible</strong></h3>



<p>In the past, it was much harder to bring a Linux phone to market, but now there are three viable types of Linux phones that can be brought to market, and all of them have better prospects than in the past. The first type is the pure Linux phone which runs a Linux kernel, like the Librem 5, Liberty, PinePhone and PinePhone Pro. These phones previously didn’t have the processing power to offer the functionality that people expect in a smartphone. They often lacked components such image signal processors, audio digital signal processors, hardware video encoders, Vulkan graphics, neural processors and encryption engines, or they lacked the FOSS drivers to support those components. The RK3588S and the upcoming <a href="https://rockchips.net/rk3688-vs-rk3668/">RK3668</a>, however, will give pure Linux phones the processing power to be viable alternatives to Android and iOS phones for the first time.</p>



<p>The pure Linux phone is the riskiest type because it requires the most investment in engineering the hardware. Companies can’t just modify a reference phone design from Qualcomm or MediaTek or call up a phone ODM and ask them to modify one of their existing designs. They have to work with a design house or ODM that has some experience with radio frequencies to custom design a phone with 2 to 3 times more components than a standard smartphone. This means many iterations of the circuit board to get it right, and some kernel work to use all those different chips. It will take a long time to get to market, which can lead to customer complaints and reputational loss if using crowdfunding to finance the dev work.</p>



<p>The benefit of the pure Linux approach is that it allows for true hardware kill switches that cut the current to separate components, rather than the use of pins on an integrated mobile SoC or software to turn off the WiFi/Bluetooth, cellular modem and GPS/GNSS. The second benefit is the ability to select components that have free/open source drivers, which allows for greater software freedom and more transparency to address security and privacy concerns. The use of proprietary blobs in the drivers often prevents the kernel from being upgraded because the blobs are often only compatible with a specific kernel version number, which allows the chipmakers to dictate the lifespan of devices. Another benefit of not using a reference design from an SoC maker or an ODM is the phone maker has full control over the design and can publish the schematics and board views for the circuit boards, so the hardware is as free as the software.</p>



<p>In the past, Qualcomm didn’t produce powerful Internet of Things processors with Linux support, but now it is possible to build a good Linux phone based on an Internet of Things processor, like the QCM6490 or QCM8850 with an integrated cellular modem. These Dragonwing processors are not as up-to-date as the latest Snapdragon processors, but most of them receive 10 years or 15 years of software support, and many of them will eventually get partial support in mainline Linux, so they can live on with community support after they are no longer supported by Qualcomm.</p>



<p>The third kind of Linux phone is built on an integrated mobile SoC, which runs a standard Linux stack on top of an Android kernel using libhybris to translate glib calls to Android’s Bionic. This used to not be a good option because Android SoC’s were not supported long enough for the long development cycles and lower annual sales of Linux phones, but now it is possible for the high-end processors that are getting 8 years of software support from Qualcomm and MediaTek.</p>



<p>This approach is the most economical way to bring a Linux phone to market, since many phone ODMs already have reference models available and are willing to work with phone sellers to tweak those models to their specifications. It is not impossible to work with an ODM to add privacy switches to a design, as Furi Labs demonstrated with its FLX1, but it takes longer to get a custom design to market, so it is important to select an SoC that gets 7 or 8 years of software support, so the phone will enjoy a reasonable lifespan.</p>



<p>It is no longer necessary for Linux phone companies to expend huge amounts of capital in developing mobile Linux, because there are now three good mobile interfaces—Phosh, Plasma Mobile and Lomiri—which offer a reasonably complete complement of applications, so hardware companies don’t need to risk bankruptcy developing the software like before. Because Phosh and now Plasma Mobile are based on a well-supported desktop stack and convergent applications, they will require much less work to maintain and it is easier and less costly to keep providing software updates for the phone. Companies offering Linux phones will still need to hire developers to work on things like power management and camera support, but they don’t have to bet the farm like Purism did with the Librem 5 or provide no software support as PINE64 did with the PinePhone and PinePhone Pro.</p>



<figure><table><tbody><tr><td colspan="9"><strong>Different approaches to designing a Linux phone</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Approach</td><td>SoC</td><td>CPU / GPU</td><td>Battery life</td><td>Support</td><td>HW dev</td><td>SW dev</td><td>Price (1-5)</td><td>Notes</td></tr><tr><td>Community Linux</td><td>RK3566</td><td>Bad</td><td>Bad</td><td>Forever</td><td>hard</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>software outsourced to the community, poor quality convergence</td></tr><tr><td>Expensive Linux</td><td>RK3588S</td><td>Sub-optimal</td><td>Poor</td><td>Forever</td><td>very hard</td><td>very hard</td><td>5</td><td>real hardware kill switches, convergence possible</td></tr><tr><td>Mid-range blobbed Linux</td><td>QCM6490</td><td>Good</td><td>Sub-optimal</td><td>till 2035</td><td>easy</td><td>hard</td><td>4</td><td>lots of blobs, convergence possible</td></tr><tr><td>Economic Android kernel</td><td>Snapdragon 4/6 Gen 4</td><td>OK</td><td>Good</td><td>4 years</td><td>easy</td><td>moderate</td><td>2</td><td>lots of blobs, no convergence</td></tr><tr><td>Mid-range Android kernel</td><td>Snapdragon 7 Gen 4</td><td>Good</td><td>Good</td><td>8 years</td><td>easy</td><td>moderate</td><td>3</td><td>lots of blobs, no convergence</td></tr><tr><td>Expensive Android kernel</td><td>Snapdragon 8 Gen 5</td><td>Stellar</td><td>OK</td><td>8 years</td><td>easy</td><td>moderate</td><td>5</td><td>lots of blobs, convergence possible</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<p>This reduction in software development costs, however, probably won’t result in more economical Linux phones. The shorter software support for the QMC4490 and the mid-range and low range Snapdragon and Dimensity processors rules out the making of an economical Linux phone. It is possible to make an upper mid-range Linux phone based on a Snapdragon 7 or Dimensity 8000 series processor, but it won’t support video out via DisplayPort Alt Mode over USB-C, so it can’t be used as a convergent PC, which is a major selling point for Linux phones. Given that the QMC6490 does support DisplayPort Alt Mode, it is probably the best option for making an upper mid-range phone.</p>



<p>Companies like Jolla, Furi Labs, Volla and Brax may choose to continue making mid-range Linux phones, but they will likely suffer from the same problem as the FLX1 and C2, where the Android kernel is unsupported and no longer getting security updates, so it is better to move up to a more expensive SoC that gets 8 years of support. Ideally, these companies will stop using MediaTek processors, and select Snapdragon processors that get partially supported in mainline Linux, so people can run a distro like Nura on them after they are no longer supported.</p>



<h3 id="the-real-barrier-holding-back-linux-phones-is-the-companies"><a></a>The real barrier holding back Linux phones is the companies</h3>



<p>Now that the available SoC’s and software are no longer holding back the making of compelling Linux phones, the real barrier to creating a viable Linux phone market are the companies to make and sell those phones. There aren’t many companies with the experience and passion to make Linux phones, which historically have lost money.</p>



<p>PINE64 recently <a href="https://www.cnx-software.com/2026/08/19/pine64-hits-pause-on-linux-devices-due-to-ram-and-emmc-shortage-high-prices/">announced</a> that it is pausing production of most of its devices due to the high prices of RAM and eMMC. Essentially PINE64 is admitting that it can’t raise the price of many of its products, because they don’t have the needed software support, so many people won’t pay elevated prices for them. It appears unlikely at this point that PINE64 will change its business model to pay for software developers to work on the kernels for its hardware, nor is it repairing its relationship with the distributions and volunteer developers who have abandoned the company, so it is unlikely to ever produce the rumored PinePhone 2 using the RK3566. In theory another community-based Linux hardware company could arise, that charges a bit more so it has the necessary funds to pay developers to work on the kernel support for the hardware, but it appears highly unlikely that another community-based hardware company like PINE64 will appear.</p>



<p>In contrast, Purism is clearly planning to produce a second version of its phone. In its July 2026 newsletter, Purism stated that it “is in the early stages of planning the Librem 5 v2 and Liberty 5 v2.” It is preparing a “crowdfunding campaign in the coming months” to “gauge customer interest and traction, which will help fund the production of our second version.” The problem is that Purism’s public reputation has been stained by its refusal to refund the Librem 5 backers who cancelled their orders. Refunding the 600 customers who are still waiting for their Librem 5 refunds would do a lot to restore the company’s public reputation, and it would cost the company less than $400k. Without taking that step, it seems unlikely that Purism will be able to crowdfund the “over $2 million” that it projects will be needed to produce its next Linux phone. Maybe Purism can charge a lesser amount (such as $99) to pre-order the phone, and then use those numbers to raise capital privately to develop the phone.</p>



<p>At any rate, the time and cost of producing the Librem 5 v2 will be much less than the first version of the phone, because Phosh and its applications have already been developed and the mainline Linux driver support for the RK3588S is already in a good state due to the last four years of work by Collabora. Hopefully, Purism will either provide funding or developers to help Collabora get the ISP and hardware video encoding working.</p>



<p>The challenge for Purism is that it also needs to finance the development of its next generation of Librem 14 notebook, Librem 11 tablet and Librem Mini. The Librem 11 and Mini are rebadged ODM designs, so they don’t require as much work, but the Librem 14 is a custom design, which requires quite a bit more work and financing to create. In addition, Purism reported in its July newsletter that it has been pre-approved to get over $50 million in funding from the CHIPS Act to develop a Librem Core-V processor based on the RISC-V architecture, which will be a huge undertaking for the tiny company which doesn’t have any prior experience in chip design. Presumably, Purism will have to raise some matching funds to get the government funding for its chip project. In other words, many projects will be competing for Purism’s attention in the future, and the Librem 5 v2 could be neglected and postponed in favor of other products.</p>



<p>Presumably Dawndrums will launch a crowdfunding campaign once it has a working prototype of its RK3588S-based phone to show to the public, but the company based in Tunis has been very tight lipped about its future plans to bring the phone to market. There has been no news from Furi Labs about a new phone model to follow the FLX1s+. The implementation of Ubuntu Touch on the BraX3 phone <a href="https://forums.ubports.com/topic/12368/i-ve-been-using-beta-version-0.9-of-ubuntu-touch-for-5-months-now-on-the-brax3">has been stalled for months</a>, and it appears that Brax Technologies is depending on the UBports community to finish the porting work. Volla Phone continues to offer Ubuntu Touch as an alternative operating system on its phones, but the German company’s primary focus is on developing its own <a href="https://volla.online/en/operating-systems/volla-os/">Volla OS</a>, which is an AOSP derivative oriented toward privacy.</p>



<p>Jolla is making the right moves to create a viable Linux phone market. The Finnish company says that it plans to move to mass production of its Jolla Phone, so the phone will be in stock, and to offer the phone to the North American market. However, many in the Linux community will be reluctant to embrace Jolla’s phones, because of the proprietary software in its Silica interface and its AppSupport (aka AlienDalvik) to run Android apps. People who believe in free and open source software won’t want to buy a phone from a company that has been violating the GPL 2.0 license by not published the kernel code for its Jolla C2 and Jolla Phone. In addition, these phones use UNISOC and MediaTek processors that don’t have any mainline Linux support, and they will probably have short lifespans, since their kernel are unlikely to ever be upgraded.</p>



<p>A critical factor in the success of Linux phones is publicizing the phones to attract the niche customers who care about Linux, software freedom, privacy or security. Liberux appears to have failed because it did such a poor job of publicizing its future NEXX phone and didn’t attract enough crowdfunding. Furi Labs similarly has done a very poor job of publicizing its phone, so that most Linux enthusiasts have never heard of the phone. Likewise, Dawndrums has done a very poor job of publicizing it development work on a future RK3588S phone. In contrast, Jolla has very effectively used social media to market its Jolla Phone to European customers.</p>



<p>There are several dozen companies that sell Linux PCs, which could potentially expand into the Linux phone market. Most of those companies, however, don’t have experience in the design of custom hardware. Most of them use standard notebook designs from one of the ODM’s that make specialty notebooks such as Clevo or Tongfang. Most of these companies also don’t have much experience doing kernel work, so they are probably better off just using a standard Android design provided by a phone ODM and installing Linux on top of Halium, so they can use a well-tested Android kernel and drivers.</p>



<p>There are also a number of companies that make Linux single board computers (SBC’s), who could potentially jump into the Linux phone market. They have more experience in working with Chinese and Taiwanese companies to design and produce custom hardware. They are better equipped to undertake the engineering challenges of entering the Linux phone market, but most of them do a poor job of engaging the community. Many companies which sell Linux SBC’s don’t have public forums for their customers and do a poor job of using social media and online advertising to promote their products.</p>



<p>Public opinion surveys show that people are increasingly aware that they are losing their privacy online. Ipsos polls found that <a href="https://www.ipsos.com/en/trust-in-the-internet-2022">79% of global respondants</a> and <a href="https://www.ipsos.com/en-us/news-polls/majority-americans-are-concerned-about-safety-and-privacy-their-personal-data">84% of American respondants</a> express worry about their online privacy. A <a href="https://www.malwarebytes.com/blog/news/2025/04/72-of-people-are-worried-their-data-is-being-misused-by-the-government-and-thats-not-all" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><u>Malwarebytes</u></a> survey found that 89% of consumers are concerned regarding corporate and AI data misuse. 62% of Americans in a <a href="https://yougov.com/en-us/articles/50756-americans-are-concerned-about-data-privacy-and-some-feel-helpless" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><u>YouGov</u></a> poll report worrying about how much personal data third parties have about them online. If Linux phones can be created that provide the same functionality and ease of use as Android or iOS phones, they could tap into that huge potential market of consumers who wish to preserve their privacy online. In order to appeal to mainstream users, Linux phones will need to support the usage of Android apps through Waydroid or a similar solution. In the same way that Murena and iodé sell AOSP phones which block third party trackers, Linux phones need to incorporate similar software.</p>



<p>The conditions may be ripe for Linux phones, but it is unclear whether the companies selling Linux phones will take advantage of those conditions to create a viable alternative to the current Android/iOS duopoly. For the sake of software freedom, online privacy and the greater longevity of mobile devices, a concerted push is needed to promote mobile Linux.</p>
					</div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Doory – Open-Source Canva (184 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://github.com/shaongitbd/doory</link>
            <guid>50049872</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 00:48:27 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://github.com/shaongitbd/doory">https://github.com/shaongitbd/doory</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50049872">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div data-hpc="true" containertiming="hpc"><article itemprop="text">

<p dir="auto">Doory is a full design editor that runs in your browser. Make posters, social posts, stories, slides,
invitations, resumes, thumbnails and business cards: start from a template or a blank page, edit everything,
and export to PNG, JPG, SVG, PDF, MP4 or GIF. <strong>Every feature is free, nobody needs an account to start, and you
can run the whole thing on your own server.</strong></p>

<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">🎬 See it in action</h2><a id="user-content--see-it-in-action" aria-label="Permalink: 🎬 See it in action" href="#-see-it-in-action"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">
  <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/shaongitbd/doory/blob/main/.github/readme/demo.gif"><img src="https://github.com/shaongitbd/doory/raw/main/.github/readme/demo.gif" alt="A real recording of Doory: picking the gig poster template, renaming the band, recolouring the dates, adding a star and opening the export menu" width="100%" data-animated-image=""></a>
</p>
<p dir="auto"><sub>A real, unedited recording: pick a template, rename the band, recolour the dates, add a star, export.</sub></p>

<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">💜 Why people pick Doory</h2><a id="user-content--why-people-pick-doory" aria-label="Permalink: 💜 Why people pick Doory" href="#-why-people-pick-doory"></a></p>
<markdown-accessiblity-table><table>
  <tbody><tr>
    <td>
      <p dir="auto"><h3 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">🆓 Free, for real</h3><a id="user-content--free-for-real" aria-label="Permalink: 🆓 Free, for real" href="#-free-for-real"></a></p>
      No Pro plan, no locked templates, no watermarks, no ads. Every tool in the editor is there for everyone,
      from background removal to video export.
    </td>
    <td>
      <p dir="auto"><h3 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">⚡ Start in one click</h3><a id="user-content--start-in-one-click" aria-label="Permalink: ⚡ Start in one click" href="#-start-in-one-click"></a></p>
      Open the editor and design. Guests' work is saved in the browser; create an account only when you want
      your designs in the cloud, and they move over in one step.
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>
      <p dir="auto"><h3 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">🏠 Yours to host</h3><a id="user-content--yours-to-host" aria-label="Permalink: 🏠 Yours to host" href="#-yours-to-host"></a></p>
      One <code>docker compose up</code> runs the app, the API, the database and live collaboration on your own
      server. Every design and upload stays under your control.
    </td>
    <td>
      <p dir="auto"><h3 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">🛠️ A real editor</h3><a id="user-content-️-a-real-editor" aria-label="Permalink: 🛠️ A real editor" href="#️-a-real-editor"></a></p>
      Layers, groups, masks, filters, curved text, snapping, rulers, multi-page designs, animation, audio,
      presenting, comments and real-time co-editing.
    </td>
  </tr>
</tbody></table></markdown-accessiblity-table>

<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">🎨 Everything you need to design</h2><a id="user-content--everything-you-need-to-design" aria-label="Permalink: 🎨 Everything you need to design" href="#-everything-you-need-to-design"></a></p>
<markdown-accessiblity-table><table>
  <tbody><tr>
    <td>
      <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/shaongitbd/doory/blob/main/.github/readme/editor.webp"><img src="https://github.com/shaongitbd/doory/raw/main/.github/readme/editor.webp" alt="The Doory editor with the gig poster template open"></a>
      <p dir="auto"><h3 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Edit anything</h3><a id="user-content-edit-anything" aria-label="Permalink: Edit anything" href="#edit-anything"></a></p>
      Click any text, shape or photo to change it. Fonts, colours, sizes, alignment and effects are one click
      away in the toolbar above the page.
    </td>
    <td>
      <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/shaongitbd/doory/blob/main/.github/readme/feature-animate.webp"><img src="https://github.com/shaongitbd/doory/raw/main/.github/readme/feature-animate.webp" alt="The Animate panel with enter, exit and emphasis animations"></a>
      <p dir="auto"><h3 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Bring it to life</h3><a id="user-content-bring-it-to-life" aria-label="Permalink: Bring it to life" href="#bring-it-to-life"></a></p>
      27 enter and exit animations, 12 emphasis effects and 16 page transitions, with music and sound effects
      from a built-in library. Present it, or export it as a video.
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>
      <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/shaongitbd/doory/blob/main/.github/readme/feature-layers.webp"><img src="https://github.com/shaongitbd/doory/raw/main/.github/readme/feature-layers.webp" alt="The Layers panel with its right-click menu"></a>
      <p dir="auto"><h3 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Stay organised</h3><a id="user-content-stay-organised" aria-label="Permalink: Stay organised" href="#stay-organised"></a></p>
      Every object is a layer you can rename, lock, hide, reorder by dragging, group, or right-click to copy,
      duplicate, delete and arrange.
    </td>
    <td>
      <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/shaongitbd/doory/blob/main/.github/readme/feature-export.webp"><img src="https://github.com/shaongitbd/doory/raw/main/.github/readme/feature-export.webp" alt="The export menu: PNG, JPG, PDF, SVG, video and GIF"></a>
      <p dir="auto"><h3 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Export anywhere</h3><a id="user-content-export-anywhere" aria-label="Permalink: Export anywhere" href="#export-anywhere"></a></p>
      PNG with transparency, JPG, SVG and PDF, plus MP4, WebM and animated GIF, all made in your browser. Need
      print? Get a print-ready PDF with bleed and crop marks.
    </td>
  </tr>
</tbody></table></markdown-accessiblity-table>
<details>
<summary><b>See the full feature list</b></summary>
<br>
<markdown-accessiblity-table><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Text</strong></td>
<td>Headings, curved text, text effects, font pairings, bulleted and numbered lists, an open-licensed font catalogue and your own uploaded fonts</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Elements</strong></td>
<td>Shapes, lines and arrows, about 25,000 icons, emoji and illustrations, stickers, QR codes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Photos</strong></td>
<td>Upload, crop, masks and filters, one-click background removal (the model runs in your browser), stock photo search with an Unsplash or Pexels key</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Layout</strong></td>
<td>Layers with lock and hide, groups, alignment guides, snapping, rulers, multi-page designs with pages of different sizes, magic resize</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Motion</strong></td>
<td>27 enter and exit animations, 12 emphasis animations and 16 page transitions on a timeline</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Sound</strong></td>
<td>A built-in library of 24 music loops and 88 sound effects (CC0), uploads, voiceover recording, trims and fades</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Present</strong></td>
<td>Present mode with speaker notes, timing, auto-advance, a laser pointer and a second presenter window</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Together</strong></td>
<td>Share links that can view, comment or edit (optionally password protected), pinned comments, version history and real-time co-editing with live cursors</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Brand</strong></td>
<td>Brand kits with your colours, fonts and logos, one click away in every design</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Export</strong></td>
<td>PNG, JPG, SVG and PDF, MP4, WebM and animated GIF, all made in the browser, plus print-ready PDF with bleed and crop marks</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></markdown-accessiblity-table>
</details>

<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">🖼️ Templates that look good from the start</h2><a id="user-content-️-templates-that-look-good-from-the-start" aria-label="Permalink: 🖼️ Templates that look good from the start" href="#️-templates-that-look-good-from-the-start"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">
  <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/shaongitbd/doory/blob/main/.github/readme/template-collage.webp"><img src="https://github.com/shaongitbd/doory/raw/main/.github/readme/template-collage.webp" alt="Ten starter templates: a gig poster, a wedding invitation, an event story, a sale post, a café flyer, a quote card, a resume, a YouTube thumbnail, a pitch deck and a business card" width="100%"></a>
</p>
<p dir="auto">Ten starter templates ship with Doory, each with its own palette, set in open-licensed fonts and fully editable:
a gig poster, a wedding invitation, an event story, a sale post, a café flyer, a quote card, a resume, a YouTube
thumbnail, a pitch deck and a business card. Add your own in the admin, and browse them all by category,
size and orientation.</p>
<p dir="auto">
  <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/shaongitbd/doory/blob/main/.github/readme/templates.webp"><img src="https://github.com/shaongitbd/doory/raw/main/.github/readme/templates.webp" alt="The template gallery" width="100%"></a>
</p>


<p dir="auto">Canva is a great product. Doory is for people who want the same kind of tool, but free, open and in their own hands.</p>
<markdown-accessiblity-table><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th><strong>Doory</strong></th>
<th>Canva</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Price</td>
<td><strong>Free, every feature</strong></td>
<td>Free plan, paid Pro and Teams plans</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Open source</td>
<td>✅</td>
<td>❌</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Run it on your own server</td>
<td>✅</td>
<td>❌</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Design without an account</td>
<td>✅</td>
<td>❌</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Your files stay on infrastructure you control</td>
<td>✅</td>
<td>❌</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Real-time co-editing and comments</td>
<td>✅</td>
<td>✅</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Animation, video and GIF export</td>
<td>✅</td>
<td>✅</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Background removal</td>
<td>✅ in your browser</td>
<td>✅</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Template library</td>
<td>10 starter templates, plus any you add</td>
<td>A very large library</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></markdown-accessiblity-table>

<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">🚀 Self-host it in 3 commands</h2><a id="user-content--self-host-it-in-3-commands" aria-label="Permalink: 🚀 Self-host it in 3 commands" href="#-self-host-it-in-3-commands"></a></p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="cp .env.example .env     # set DJANGO_SECRET_KEY (openssl rand -base64 48) and POSTGRES_PASSWORD
docker compose up -d --build
docker compose exec api python manage.py createsuperuser"><pre>cp .env.example .env     <span><span>#</span> set DJANGO_SECRET_KEY (openssl rand -base64 48) and POSTGRES_PASSWORD</span>
docker compose up -d --build
docker compose <span>exec</span> api python manage.py createsuperuser</pre></div>
<p dir="auto">Open <a href="http://localhost:8080/" rel="nofollow">http://localhost:8080</a> and start designing. The <code>web</code> container serves the app and forwards <code>/api</code> and
<code>/ws</code> to the backend, so the browser only ever talks to one address.</p>
<details>
<summary><b>Add the starter content</b> (templates, icons, fonts, audio)</summary>

<p dir="auto">A new site starts empty. Add whatever you want once it is running:</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="docker compose exec api python manage.py seed_gallery         # template categories
docker compose exec api python manage.py sync_fonts --popular 100
docker compose exec api python manage.py seed_templates       # the starter templates (add --update to refresh them)
docker compose exec api python manage.py seed_elements        # shapes and graphics
docker compose exec api python manage.py sync_element_packs   # icons, emoji and illustrations (download)
docker compose exec api python manage.py sync_models          # background removal model
docker compose exec api python manage.py sync_audio           # music and sound effects"><pre>docker compose <span>exec</span> api python manage.py seed_gallery         <span><span>#</span> template categories</span>
docker compose <span>exec</span> api python manage.py sync_fonts --popular 100
docker compose <span>exec</span> api python manage.py seed_templates       <span><span>#</span> the starter templates (add --update to refresh them)</span>
docker compose <span>exec</span> api python manage.py seed_elements        <span><span>#</span> shapes and graphics</span>
docker compose <span>exec</span> api python manage.py sync_element_packs   <span><span>#</span> icons, emoji and illustrations (download)</span>
docker compose <span>exec</span> api python manage.py sync_models          <span><span>#</span> background removal model</span>
docker compose <span>exec</span> api python manage.py sync_audio           <span><span>#</span> music and sound effects</span></pre></div>
</details>
<details>
<summary><b>Settings worth knowing</b> (<code>.env</code>)</summary>
<br>
<markdown-accessiblity-table><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Setting</th>
<th>What it does</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>SITE_URL</code></td>
<td>The public address of the site, e.g. <code>https://doory.example.com</code> (no trailing slash)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS</code></td>
<td>The host names the site answers to, comma separated</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>SECURE_SSL_REDIRECT</code>, <code>SECURE_COOKIES</code></td>
<td><code>true</code> behind HTTPS, <code>false</code> for plain <code>http://</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>EMAIL_URL</code>, <code>DEFAULT_FROM_EMAIL</code></td>
<td>SMTP, e.g. <code>smtp+tls://user:password@smtp.example.com:587</code> (without it, emails are printed to the API log)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>WEB_PORT</code>, <code>WEB_BIND</code></td>
<td>Where the site is published (default <code>0.0.0.0:8080</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>INSTALL_CHROMIUM</code></td>
<td><code>false</code> skips the headless browser that server-side PDF export needs</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID</code></td>
<td>Turns on "Sign in with Google"</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>UNSPLASH_ACCESS_KEY</code>, <code>PEXELS_API_KEY</code></td>
<td>Turn on stock photo search</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>USE_S3</code> and <code>AWS_*</code></td>
<td>Store uploads in S3-compatible storage instead of a local volume</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></markdown-accessiblity-table>
</details>
<details>
<summary><b>Develop locally</b></summary>
<br>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="make setup     # virtualenv, dependencies, local database
make dev-api   # API on http://localhost:8000
make dev-web   # app on http://localhost:5173 (second terminal)"><pre>make setup     <span><span>#</span> virtualenv, dependencies, local database</span>
make dev-api   <span><span>#</span> API on http://localhost:8000</span>
make dev-web   <span><span>#</span> app on http://localhost:5173 (second terminal)</span></pre></div>
<p dir="auto"><code>make help</code> lists the other targets (tests, lint, content).</p>
</details>

<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">❓ FAQ</h2><a id="user-content--faq" aria-label="Permalink: ❓ FAQ" href="#-faq"></a></p>
<details>
<summary><b>Is it really free?</b></summary>
<br>
Yes. There are no plans, no paywalls and no ads in Doory. Every feature is available to everyone.
</details>
<details>
<summary><b>Do I need an account?</b></summary>
<br>
No. Guests design straight away and their work is saved in the browser. An account keeps designs in the cloud,
lets you share them and use brand kits; signing up moves your guest designs over in one step.
</details>
<details>
<summary><b>Can I use it for my business or host it for my users?</b></summary>
<br>
Yes, as long as you credit the author as the licence asks: keep the licence and NOTICE with it and show the
credit in the running app. See <a href="#-license-and-credit">License and credit</a>.
</details>
<details>
<summary><b>Where are my designs stored?</b></summary>
<br>
Guest designs and their images live in the browser on that device. Signed-in designs are stored on the server
that runs Doory: yours, if you self-host. Uploads can go to a local volume or to S3-compatible storage.
</details>

<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">🧱 Built with</h2><a id="user-content--built-with" aria-label="Permalink: 🧱 Built with" href="#-built-with"></a></p>
